{"questions":[{"_i":0,"q":"55-year-old man: pressing chest pain radiating to the left arm, sweating, nausea. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["STEMI","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Pancreatitis"],"ex":"Classic STEMI presentation: pressing pain, radiation, autonomic symptoms. Immediate PCI within 90 min.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Acute myocardial infarction due to complete occlusion of a coronary artery. Every minute of delay means more irreversible myocardial loss.","wiki_redflag":"Women and people with diabetes present atypically — nausea and fatigue without chest pain.","wiki_bigfact_label":"Door-to-balloon time","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Maximum time from ED arrival to opening the coronary artery by PCI.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Plaque rupture","desc":"Atherosclerotic plaque ruptures, exposing subendothelial collagen."},{"title":"Thrombus formation","desc":"Platelets aggregate at the rupture site and completely occlude the artery."},{"title":"Transmural infarction","desc":"The full thickness of the myocardium becomes necrotic — ST elevation on ECG, troponin rises after 3–6 h."},{"title":"Irreversible damage","desc":"Begins as early as 20–40 minutes. Every minute ≈ 2 g of myocardium lost."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"STEMI","desc":"ST elevation in ≥2 contiguous leads, regional distribution. Immediate PCI."},{"label":"Aortic dissection","desc":"Thrombolysis is fatal here — always rule out with asymmetry and tearing pain."},{"label":"NSTEMI","desc":"No ST elevation but troponin is elevated. PCI within 24–72 h."},{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"Diffuse saddle-shaped ST elevation, pain worse when lying down, no regional distribution."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate PCI within 90 minutes — this is the only metric that counts.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Dual antiplatelet therapy","detail":"Aspirin 300 mg + ticagrelor 180 mg immediately on arrival."},{"naam":"IV heparin","detail":"Bridging until PCI — prevents further thrombus growth."},{"naam":"No PCI available?","detail":"Thrombolysis within 30 minutes after arrival as an alternative."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"Statin + beta-blocker + ACE inhibitor — always after discharge."}]},{"_i":1,"q":"ECG: no P waves, irregular rhythm with "sawtooth" baseline. Rate 150/min. Diagnosis?","a":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter","Ventricular tachycardia","WPW syndrome"],"ex":"Atrial flutter: F waves (sawtooth), typically regular 2:1 block → 150/min. AF is instead irregular without F waves.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Atrial flutter is an organized re-entry circuit in the right atrium (~300/min) with typical 2:1 AV block → ventricular rate exactly ~150/min, regular.","wiki_redflag":"A regular rhythm of exactly 150/min is atrial flutter until proven otherwise — not sinus tachycardia.","wiki_bigfact_label":"Ventricular rate","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Regular 150/min due to 2:1 AV block — the "magic number" that suggests flutter.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Re-entry circuit","desc":"Electrical impulse circles in the right atrium via the cavotricuspid isthmus (~300/min)."},{"title":"F waves","desc":"Sawtooth pattern visible in II, III, aVF and V1 — characteristically negative in inferior leads."},{"title":"AV block","desc":"The AV node does not conduct every impulse: typically 2:1 → 150/min; with medication 3:1 or 4:1."},{"title":"Carotid sinus massage","desc":"Increases vagal tone → temporarily slows AV conduction → F waves become visible."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Atrial flutter","desc":"Regular ~150/min, sawtooth F waves in II/III/aVF, revealed by carotid sinus massage."},{"label":"Atrial fibrillation","desc":"Absolutely irregular, no F waves, chaotic baseline — never exactly 150/min."},{"label":"Sinus tachycardia","desc":"Regular but P waves visible before every QRS, rate varies with respiration."},{"label":"AVNRT","desc":"Narrow-complex tachycardia, sudden onset/termination, P waves hidden in or just after QRS."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Rate control with a beta-blocker or non-DHP calcium channel blocker (verapamil/diltiazem).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Cardioversion","detail":"Electrically effective with low energy (50 J); flutter is easier to cardiovert than AF."},{"naam":"Catheter ablation","detail":">95% curative success by interrupting the cavotricuspid isthmus — first choice in recurrence."},{"naam":"Anticoagulation","detail":"Same indications as AF (CHA₂DS₂-VASc) — thrombotic risk is comparable."}]},{"_i":2,"q":"68-year-old woman: shortness of breath on exertion, bilateral ankle edema, elevated JVP, basal crackles. Diagnosis?","a":["Pulmonary embolism","Decompensated heart failure","COPD exacerbation","Nephrotic syndrome"],"ex":"Decompensated heart failure: left-sided congestion (crackles, orthopnea) and right-sided congestion (ankles, elevated JVP). Distinguish from COPD: note elevated JVP and S3 gallop. Treatment: IV furosemide + sitting upright + O₂.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Decompensated heart failure is the inability of the heart to deliver sufficient cardiac output — fluid accumulates in the lungs (left) and/or systemic circulation (right). Left-sided decompensation causes orthopnea and crackles; right-sided decompensation causes ankle edema and elevated JVP.","wiki_redflag":"COPD exacerbation and heart failure resemble each other — elevated JVP, S3 gallop and crackles point specifically to heart failure.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Reduced cardiac output","desc":"Heart pumps insufficiently → RAAS and sympathetic nervous system activate as compensation."},{"title":"RAAS activation","desc":"Renin → angiotensin II → aldosterone → salt and water retention → more filling, higher filling pressure."},{"title":"Pulmonary edema (left)","desc":"Increased left atrial pressure → congestion in pulmonary veins → transudate in alveoli → crackles."},{"title":"Peripheral edema (right)","desc":"Increased right atrial pressure → congestion of neck veins, liver, ankles."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Decompensated heart failure","desc":"Bilateral crackles, elevated JVP, S3 gallop, orthopnea. BNP strongly elevated."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Sudden dyspnea but no crackles, pleuritic pain, D-dimer elevated."},{"label":"COPD exacerbation","desc":"Prolonged expiration, wheezing, no elevated JVP. BNP normal or mildly elevated."},{"label":"Pneumonia","desc":"Fever, unilateral infiltrates, purulent sputum. No elevated JVP."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Sit upright + oxygen + IV furosemide 40–80 mg — act within minutes.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Nitrates","detail":"If systolic BP >100 mmHg: isosorbide dinitrate sublingual or IV — rapid afterload reduction."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Diuresis (target >0.5 ml/kg/h), saturation, BP every 15 min — titrate furosemide to response."},{"naam":"Maintenance therapy","detail":"Start or optimize HFrEF medication after stabilization: ACE inhibitor, beta-blocker, MRA, SGLT2i."}]},{"_i":3,"q":"45-year-old man: sudden "tearing" interscapular pain, blood pressure asymmetry > 20 mmHg. Urgent diagnosis?","a":["STEMI","Aortic dissection","Pulmonary embolism","Pericardial tamponade"],"ex":"Aortic dissection: tearing character, interscapular, blood pressure asymmetry. CT angiography is diagnostic. Life-threatening.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Aortic dissection is a tear in the intima of the aortic wall in which blood creates a false lumen. Mortality rises 1–2% per hour without treatment — this is the most urgent cardiovascular emergency.","wiki_redflag":"Always rule out aortic dissection before thrombolysis — thrombolysis in dissection is fatal due to bleeding risk into the false lumen.","wiki_bigfact_label":"Mortality per hour","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Type A without surgery: 50% die within 48 hours.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Intimal tear","desc":"Hypertension or connective tissue disease (Marfan) weakens the aortic wall → tear under systolic pressure."},{"title":"False lumen","desc":"Blood enters between intima and media → false lumen expands and compresses the true lumen."},{"title":"Organ ischemia","desc":"Branch arteries (coronary, carotid, renal, mesenteric) are occluded → MI, stroke, kidney failure."},{"title":"Type A vs B","desc":"Type A (60%): ascending aorta involved → risk of tamponade, AI, coronary ischemia → surgery. Type B: descending → medical management."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Aortic dissection","desc":"Tearing pain, blood pressure asymmetry >20 mmHg between arms, widened mediastinum on chest X-ray."},{"label":"STEMI","desc":"Pressing pain, radiation to jaw/arm, ST elevations on ECG, no blood pressure asymmetry."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Pleuritic pain, dyspnea, D-dimer elevated, no pain in the back."},{"label":"Aortic aneurysm","desc":"Chronic dilation without acute tear — pain only with expansion or rupture."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Type A → emergency surgery indication. Type B → IV beta-blocker labetalol/esmolol, target systolic BP <120 mmHg.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Diagnostics","detail":"CT angiography of the aorta is the gold standard — confirms diagnosis and distinguishes type A vs B."},{"naam":"Pain control","detail":"IV morphine for adequate analgesia — reduces sympathetic activation and aortic pressure."},{"naam":"Type B complications","detail":"TEVAR (endovascular stent) for ischemia, expansion or rupture if medical therapy fails."}]},{"_i":4,"q":"Digoxin increases heart rate and is indicated for tachycardias to make the heart pump faster.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Digoxin lowers HR (negative chronotropic) via vagal stimulation. Used in AF with rapid ventricular response.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Digoxin inhibits Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → intracellular Na⁺ ↑ → Ca²⁺ ↑ → stronger contraction (positive inotropic) and stimulates the vagus nerve → slower AV node conduction (negative chronotropic). It lowers heart rate; it does not raise it.","wiki_redflag":"Hypokalemia increases toxicity risk tenfold — always keep K⁺ >3.5 mmol/L during digoxin use.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase inhibition","desc":"Digoxin blocks the sodium pump → intracellular Na⁺ rises → Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger works less → Ca²⁺ accumulates."},{"title":"Positive inotropic","desc":"More intracellular Ca²⁺ → stronger muscle contraction → higher cardiac output in heart failure."},{"title":"Negative chronotropic","desc":"Increased vagal tone inhibits the AV node → slower ventricular response in AF."},{"title":"Narrow therapeutic window","desc":"Therapeutic: 0.5–2.0 ng/ml. Toxic: >2 ng/ml. Signs: nausea, bradycardia, visual halos."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Digoxin","desc":"Negative chronotropic via vagus + positive inotropic. Advantage in AF with heart failure (low EF)."},{"label":"Beta-blockers","desc":"More effective for rate control in AF, no inotropic effect. First choice in AF without heart failure."},{"label":"Verapamil/diltiazem","desc":"Non-DHP calcium channel blockers: also rate control but negative inotropic — contraindicated in low EF."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop digoxin with signs of toxicity — digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Fab) for severe toxicity.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Correct potassium","detail":"Always keep K⁺ >3.5 — hypokalemia dramatically increases toxicity risk."},{"naam":"Monitor renal function","detail":"Digoxin is renally cleared — dose reduction in renal insufficiency."},{"naam":"Level measurement","detail":"Measure at least 6 h after intake; therapeutic window 0.5–2.0 ng/ml."}]},{"_i":5,"q":"A normal ECG completely rules out an acute myocardial infarction.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. In NSTEMI or posterior infarction, the ECG can initially be normal. Troponins are essential.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"A normal ECG does NOT rule out an acute myocardial infarction. NSTEMI, posterior MI and early-stage infarction can show a normal or subtle ECG. Serial high-sensitivity troponin is the cornerstone of diagnosis.","wiki_redflag":"Posterior MI (LCx occlusion): ST depression V1-V3 is the only ECG sign — additional V7-V9 leads are needed for diagnosis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"NSTEMI: no ST elevation","desc":"In NSTEMI, the coronary occlusion is subtotal or intermittent → no transmural infarction → no ST elevation. ST depression and T inversion may occur, but the ECG can also be completely normal."},{"title":"Posterior MI","desc":"The left circumflex artery (LCx) supplies the posterior myocardium — this area is not directly visible in standard leads. Only sign: reciprocal ST depression in V1-V3 and tall R in V1."},{"title":"Time window","desc":"ECG changes may not occur until 15-30 minutes. A first ECG on arrival may be normal while the infarction is just beginning."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Normal ECG does NOT rule out MI","desc":"With high clinical suspicion, always obtain hs-troponin (0+1 h or 0+3 h protocol). Normal ECG + low troponin = MI unlikely."},{"label":"STEMI","desc":"ST elevation ≥1 mm in ≥2 contiguous leads — transmural, immediate PCI <90 min."},{"label":"NSTEMI","desc":"Troponin elevated without ST elevation. ST depression or T inversion possible, but ECG may also be normal."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Always measure serial troponin in chest pain, regardless of the ECG.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"0/1 h protocol","detail":"Hs-troponin on arrival + after 1 hour: significant rise (delta) = rule-in ACS."},{"naam":"Posterior leads","detail":"Obtain V7-V9 when LCx occlusion is suspected (ST depression V1-V3 + tall R in V1)."}]},{"_i":6,"q":"72-year-old man: sudden weakness of right arm/leg and speech impairment. Fully recovered after 45 minutes. Diagnosis?","a":["Cerebral infarction","TIA","Epileptic seizure","Migraine with aura"],"ex":"TIA resolves by definition within 24 h. High recurrence risk: determine ABCD2 score and treat immediately.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"A TIA is an episode of focal neurological deficit due to temporary ischemia, fully resolving within 24 h (usually <1 h). It is a serious warning sign: 10-15% have a stroke within 3 months, with the risk highest in the first 48 h.","wiki_redflag":"After a TIA, stroke risk is highest in the first 48 h — ABCD2 score ≥4 requires immediate admission and workup, not delayed outpatient evaluation.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Embolic cause","desc":"Cardiac emboli (AF, prosthetic valves, LV thrombus), large-vessel atherosclerosis (internal carotid artery) or lacunar ischemia in hypertension."},{"title":"Temporary occlusion","desc":"Blood clot dissolves spontaneously → complete recovery. MRI-DWI still shows a diffusion-positive small infarct in 30-50%: these are actually small strokes."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"TIA","desc":"Focal deficit fully resolving within 24 h. ABCD2 score determines urgency. High recurrence risk: first 48 h most dangerous."},{"label":"Cerebral infarction (stroke)","desc":"Same presentation but deficit persists after 24 h, DWI-positive on MRI — immediate thrombolysis or thrombectomy if possible."},{"label":"Epileptic seizure","desc":"Tonic-clonic or focal motor deficit with postictal phase — EEG confirms, no vascular-territory distribution."},{"label":"Migraine with aura","desc":"Deficit builds gradually (20-30 min, spreading), visual phenomena dominant, followed by headache. Never exclusively "negative" deficits."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Aspirin 300 mg immediately + blood pressure control. Do not wait.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ABCD2 score ≥4","detail":"Admission: ECG + Holter (rule out AF), carotid ultrasound, echocardiography."},{"naam":"AF demonstrated","detail":"Anticoagulation (DOAC) instead of antiplatelet therapy — dramatically lower recurrence risk."},{"naam":">70% carotid stenosis","detail":"Carotid endarterectomy within 2 weeks after TIA (evidence-based)."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"Optimize antihypertensive + high-intensity statin + antidiabetic therapy."}]},{"_i":7,"q":"45-year-old woman: neck stiffness, photophobia, fever 39.5°C, positive Kernig sign. Urgency?","a":["Migraine","Subarachnoid hemorrhage","Bacterial meningitis","Encephalitis"],"ex":"Meningeal irritation + fever = bacterial meningitis until proven otherwise. Immediate antibiotics, do not wait for LP.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Bacterial meningitis is a life-threatening inflammation of the meninges. The classic triad: fever + neck stiffness + photophobia/phonophobia. The fastest treatment saves lives — antibiotics immediately, never wait.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"Antibiotics before LP","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Delaying antibiotics significantly increases mortality — start before lumbar puncture if LP cannot be done immediately.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Bacterial invasion","desc":"S. pneumoniae (adults) and N. meningitidis (young people) reach the subarachnoid space via the hematogenous route — local immunity is weak."},{"title":"Inflammation + edema","desc":"Massive inflammatory response → cerebral vessels become inflamed → cortical ischemia + increased intracranial pressure."},{"title":"Brain injury","desc":"Increased ICP + vasculitis → infarction. Dexamethasone before antibiotics inhibits TNF-α and reduces this injury."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Bacterial meningitis","desc":"High fever, neck stiffness, PMN pleocytosis in CSF, low glucose (<40% serum), high protein (>1 g/L). Signs of sepsis possible."},{"label":"Viral meningitis","desc":"Milder, lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal glucose, slightly elevated protein — self-limiting, no antibiotics needed."},{"label":"HSV encephalitis","desc":"Impaired consciousness + behavioral change (brain parenchyma involved), temporal MRI abnormalities — start acyclovir immediately."},{"label":"SAH","desc":"Thunderclap headache but fever absent, xanthochromia in CSF, no pleocytosis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Ceftriaxone 2 g IV + dexamethasone 10 mg IV — start dexamethasone before or simultaneously with antibiotics.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Blood cultures","detail":"Obtain before antibiotics, but do NOT delay antibiotics — start immediately if suspicion is high."},{"naam":"CT and LP","detail":"CT first in decreased consciousness/papilledema/focal deficit; otherwise immediate LP for CSF diagnosis."},{"naam":"Isolation","detail":"Droplet precautions for the first 24 h in N. meningitidis — no longer contagious afterward."},{"naam":"Contact prophylaxis","detail":"Rifampicin or ciprofloxacin for household members and close contacts in meningococcal disease."}]},{"_i":8,"q":"28-year-old woman: visual disturbances, then leg weakness, months later tingling in arm. MRI: lesions disseminated in time and space. Which diagnosis fits best?","a":["ALS","Multiple sclerosis","Guillain-Barré","Myasthenia gravis"],"ex":"MS: demyelinating disease, lesions disseminated in time and space. Typical in young women (F:M = 3:1).","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a chronic autoimmune demyelinating disease of the central nervous system. Autoreactive T cells damage myelin → slowed or blocked nerve conduction.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Myelin attack","desc":"Autoreactive T cells cross the blood-brain barrier and attack oligodendrocytes (myelin-producing cells) → slowed or blocked nerve conduction."},{"title":"After demyelination","desc":"partial recovery through remyelination, but also axonal damage."},{"title":"During relapses","desc":"active inflammation."},{"title":"Progressive phase","desc":"chronic neurodestruction without active inflammation."},{"title":"Relapse triggers","desc":"infections, fever, stress."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Multiple sclerosis","desc":"Lesions separated in time and space. McDonald criteria: MRI periventricular/juxtacortical + oligoclonal bands in CSF. Relapsing-remitting in 85%."},{"label":"NMO (neuromyelitis optica)","desc":"More severe attacks, longitudinal myelitis >3 vertebrae, optic neuritis, AQP4 antibodies positive. Does NOT respond to MS DMTs."},{"label":"ADEM","desc":"Single episode after infection or vaccination, children, multifocal MRI lesions, treatment with corticosteroids."},{"label":"Vascular white matter abnormalities","desc":"Periventricular lesions in older adults with vascular risk factors — no attacks, no oligoclonal bands."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Relapse: IV methylprednisolone 3-5 days (shortens duration, no long-term effect).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Disease-modifying therapy (DMT)","detail":"interferon-beta, glatiramer acetate (mild MS) or natalizumab, ocrelizumab (active/severe MS)."},{"naam":"Symptomatic treatment","detail":"baclofen (spasticity), amantadine (fatigue), SSRI (depression)."}]},{"_i":9,"q":"In a stroke, a CT scan must always be performed before thrombolysis to rule out hemorrhage.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Thrombolysis in hemorrhagic stroke is fatal. Always CT first. "Time is brain" — but safety comes first.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Thrombolysis in a bleeding stroke is immediately fatal — it dissolves clots and worsens the hemorrhage. CT before alteplase is an absolute requirement, not optional.","wiki_redflag":"Thrombolysis in hemorrhagic stroke greatly increases mortality. Never start without imaging that rules out hemorrhage.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Ischemic stroke","desc":"Thrombotic or embolic occlusion of a cerebral artery → ischemia → thrombolysis dissolves clot → reperfusion."},{"title":"Hemorrhagic stroke","desc":"Blood vessel has already ruptured → thrombolysis dislodges clot and enlarges hemorrhage → fatal herniation."},{"title":"CT before thrombolysis","desc":"Blood appears as hyperdensity on CT (white) in acute hematoma — rules out hemorrhage before treatment."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Ischemic stroke (85%)","desc":"CT normal in first 6 h. Thrombolysis ≤4.5 h from symptom onset. Embolic: atrial fibrillation."},{"label":"Hemorrhagic stroke (15%)","desc":"CT: hyperdense hemorrhage. No thrombolysis. Consider blood pressure treatment + neurosurgery."},{"label":"TIA","desc":"Complete recovery <24 h (mostly <1 h). High subsequent stroke risk — start DAPT and statin immediately."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate CT brain → if ischemic: alteplase 0.9 mg/kg IV ≤4.5 h after symptom onset.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Thrombolysis contraindications","detail":"Recent surgery, bleeding diathesis, BP >185/110, glucose <2.7 or >22, oral anticoagulants."},{"naam":"Thrombectomy","detail":"For large-vessel occlusion (M1/ICA): mechanical thrombectomy up to 24 h in selected patients."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"Aspirin + statin immediately after excluding hemorrhage. Anticoagulants in AF."}]},{"_i":10,"q":"34-year-old man, 2 weeks after gastroenteritis:\nProgressive ascending muscle weakness, areflexia\nCSF: protein 2.8 g/L, cells 3/µL\nDiagnosis?","a":["Multiple sclerosis","ALS","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Myasthenia gravis"],"ex":"GBS: ascending motor deficit + areflexia after infection + albuminocytologic dissociation (high protein, almost no cells). Respiratory monitoring is essential — 30% require ventilation. Treatment: IVIG or plasmapheresis.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Guillain-Barré syndrome is an autoimmune demyelinating disorder of the peripheral nerves, classically 2-4 weeks after infection (Campylobacter, CMV, EBV). Key feature: ascending paralysis — starts in the legs.","wiki_redflag":"30% of GBS patients require ventilation. Monitor vital capacity: if FVC <20 ml/kg or rapidly falling → intubation. Respiratory failure is the leading cause of death.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Molecular mimicry","desc":"Antibodies directed against bacterial/viral structures cross-react with gangliosides on peripheral nerve myelin."},{"title":"Demyelination","desc":"Impulse conduction slows or blocks → ascending weakness + areflexia + sensory disturbances."},{"title":"Albuminocytologic dissociation","desc":"CSF: markedly elevated protein (>1 g/L) but almost no cells (<10/µL) — characteristic of GBS."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Guillain-Barré (GBS)","desc":"Ascending paralysis + areflexia + albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF, 2-4 weeks after infection."},{"label":"Myasthenia gravis (MG)","desc":"Also muscle weakness but fatigability pattern (worse in the evening), no areflexia, sensation intact."},{"label":"Botulism","desc":"Descending paralysis (top to bottom!), abnormal pupillary responses, no fever, contaminated food."},{"label":"Transverse myelitis","desc":"Spinal level identifiable, UMN signs, bladder/bowel involved early."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IVIG 2 g/kg over 5 days OR plasmapheresis — equally effective.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"No corticosteroids","detail":"Corticosteroids are NOT effective in GBS — they even prolong recovery time."},{"naam":"Monitor breathing","detail":"FVC every 4-6 h. If FVC <20 ml/kg or rapidly falling → elective intubation."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"80% complete recovery after 6-12 months. 5% remain severely disabled."}]},{"_i":11,"q":"70-year-old woman: memory loss, urge incontinence, broad-based gait. CT: ventricular enlargement without cortical atrophy. Diagnosis?","a":["Alzheimer's disease","Normal-pressure hydrocephalus (NPH)","Lewy body dementia","Vascular dementia"],"ex":"NPH Hakim triad: dementia + incontinence + gait disturbance ("wacky, wet, wobbly"). CT: large ventricles, little cortical atrophy. Lumbar puncture as diagnostic/therapeutic test — improvement after CSF drainage confirms diagnosis.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Normal-pressure hydrocephalus (NPH) is a treatable cause of dementia. Hakim triad: gait disturbance (broad-based, "magnetic") + urge incontinence + cognitive decline. CT: large ventricles without cortical atrophy.","wiki_redflag":"NPH is treatable — do not miss it! Confusion with Alzheimer's is the most common mistake. Key difference: CT shows disproportionate ventricular enlargement without cortical atrophy.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Impaired CSF resorption","desc":"CSF is insufficiently reabsorbed (arachnoid villi) → ventricular pressure rises chronically → periventricular white matter is damaged."},{"title":"Triad","desc":"Frontal white matter pressure → gait disturbance (frontal gait pattern). Pericallosal fiber pressure → bladder incontinence. Frontal cortex pressure → cognitive slowing."},{"title":"Tap test","desc":"LP with drainage of 30-50 ml CSF → temporary improvement in gait/cognition confirms diagnosis and predicts success of ventricular shunt."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"NPH","desc":"Hakim triad + CT large ventricles without atrophy + positive tap test. Treatable with ventricular shunt."},{"label":"Alzheimer's disease","desc":"No early gait disturbance, no early incontinence. CT: temporoparietal atrophy. Not treatable with shunt."},{"label":"Vascular dementia","desc":"Stepwise course, cardiovascular risk factors, white matter abnormalities on MRI."},{"label":"Parkinson's disease","desc":"Resting tremor + rigidity + bradykinesia. Gait is shuffling but narrow, not broad-based."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt in tap-test-positive NPH.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Tap test","detail":"LP 30-50 ml CSF drainage → measure gait improvement after 24-48 h. Positive = strong predictor of shunt success."},{"naam":"Shunt placement","detail":"VP shunt drains excess CSF from ventricles to abdominal cavity — improves triad in 70-80% of good candidates."},{"naam":"Repeat LPs","detail":"In patients with shunt risk: serial LPs as temporary treatment."}]},{"_i":12,"q":"In an epidural hematoma, there is typically a lucid interval of hours between the initial trauma and decreased consciousness.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Epidural hematoma (middle meningeal artery): brief loss of consciousness → lucid interval (arterial bleeding slowly builds pressure) → rapid deterioration. Emergency neurosurgical intervention is life-saving.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Epidural hematoma is an arterial hemorrhage between skull and dura (middle meningeal artery with temporal fracture). The lucid interval — the patient seems to recover — is the classic feature and gives a false sense of safety.","wiki_redflag":"Ipsilateral pupillary dilation + contralateral hemiparesis = transtentorial herniation. Emergency neurosurgical evacuation is then the only life-saving intervention.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Skull trauma","desc":"Temporal fracture ruptures the middle meningeal artery — arterial pressure pumps blood into the epidural space."},{"title":"Lucid interval","desc":"Patient appears to recover while arterial bleeding slowly builds pressure — characteristic of epidural hematoma."},{"title":"Herniation","desc":"After hours, the hematoma reaches critical pressure → transtentorial herniation → ipsilateral pupillary dilation, contralateral hemiparesis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Epidural hematoma","desc":"Biconvex on CT, arterial, lucid interval, young people after temporal trauma. Neurosurgical emergency."},{"label":"Subdural hematoma","desc":"Crescent-shaped on CT, venous (bridging veins), older adults/anticoagulation, slower course."},{"label":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","desc":"No trauma required, thunderclap headache, no lucid interval, xanthochromia in CSF."},{"label":"Diffuse axonal injury","desc":"Coma immediately after trauma, no focal deficit, CT often normal."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Emergency neurosurgical craniotomy — evacuate hematoma as soon as herniation threatens.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Immediate head CT","detail":"Biconvex temporal hyperdensity — diagnostic. No LP required."},{"naam":"Monitor ICP","detail":"Mannitol 20% 1 g/kg IV as bridge in decreased consciousness before OR."},{"naam":"Prognosis","detail":"Early evacuation before herniation: >90% good recovery. After herniation: mortality 30-50%."}]},{"_i":13,"q":"38-year-old woman: sudden "thunderclap headache", maximum intensity reached within seconds, neck stiffness, no fever. CT brain negative. Next step?","a":["MRI brain with gadolinium","Lumbar puncture after 6-12 hours","Reassure — probably migraine","Temporal artery biopsy"],"ex":"Thunderclap + negative CT: rule out subarachnoid hemorrhage! CT misses 5% of SAH in the first hours. LP after ≥6 h detects xanthochromia. "Worst headache of life" = SAH until proven otherwise. LP is always mandatory.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) is bleeding in the space between the meninges, usually due to rupture of a cerebral aneurysm. The "thunderclap headache" — maximum intensity within seconds — is the classic alarm sign.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Aneurysm rupture","desc":"80% caused by rupture of a saccular (berry) aneurysm at bifurcations of the circle of Willis."},{"title":"Consequences of bleeding","desc":"Blood in the subarachnoid space irritates the meninges (neck stiffness), increases intracranial pressure (headache, decreased consciousness) and causes vasospasm (ischemia days 4-14)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)","desc":"Thunderclap headache maximal within seconds, neck stiffness, negative CT does NOT rule out SAH — always LP after 6-12 h for xanthochromia."},{"label":"Migraine","desc":"Gradual build-up (20-60 min), previous episodes, no thunderclap, no neck stiffness. Never first attack "worst ever" without LP exclusion."},{"label":"Bacterial meningitis","desc":"Fever more prominent, slower onset over hours, PMN pleocytosis in CSF."},{"label":"Epidural hematoma","desc":"Preceding trauma, lucid interval, CT diagnostic."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate CT brain — sensitive in first 6 h (98%). Then LP if CT is negative.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"LP after 6-12 h","detail":"Xanthochromia (yellow fluid from hemoglobin breakdown) proves SAH with negative CT. Spectrophotometry is the gold standard."},{"naam":"Aneurysm treatment","detail":"Endovascular coiling (first choice) or neurosurgical clipping as soon as possible."},{"naam":"Nimodipine","detail":"Calcium channel blocker 60 mg every 4 hours for 21 days — reduces vasospasm and cerebral ischemia (days 4-14)."},{"naam":"ICP monitoring","detail":"Hydrocephalus is a frequent complication — consider ventricular drainage if deterioration occurs."}]},{"_i":14,"q":"55-year-old man: progressive dysphagia, dysarthria, tongue fasciculations and spasticity with hyperreflexia in the legs, no sensory disturbances. Diagnosis?","a":["Myasthenia gravis","ALS (amyotrophic lateral sclerosis)","Brainstem infarction","Guillain-Barré syndrome"],"ex":"ALS: simultaneous UMN (hyperreflexia, spasticity) AND LMN signs (atrophy, fasciculations). Bulbar variant: dysphagia/dysarthria as starting point. No sensory deficit — that distinguishes ALS from GBS and MS.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive neurodegenerative disease in which both upper (UMN) and lower (LMN) motor neurons die. Median survival is 3-5 years.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Neuron loss","desc":"UMN (motor cortex) + LMN (anterior horn spinal cord + brainstem) die. Combination gives a unique picture: hyperreflexia (UMN) + fasciculations (LMN)."},{"title":"Multifactorial cause","desc":"SOD1 mutations (familial ALS), TDP-43 aggregates, RNA metabolism disorders, glutamate excitotoxicity. 90% sporadic."},{"title":"No recovery","desc":"Motor neurons do not regenerate — progression is inexorable. Sensory neurons are spared (diagnostic feature)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ALS","desc":"Simultaneous UMN (hyperreflexia, spasticity) + LMN (fasciculations, atrophy) without sensory deficit. Bulbar ALS: dysarthria + dysphagia as presentation."},{"label":"GBS","desc":"Peripheral, sensory deficit present, reversible after weeks-months, areflexia, albuminocytologic dissociation in CSF."},{"label":"Myasthenia gravis","desc":"Fatigable weakness, no fasciculations/atrophy, sensory intact, improves on pyridostigmine."},{"label":"MS","desc":"Relapsing course, sensation affected, MRI demyelinating lesions. No fasciculations."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Riluzole 50 mg 2×/day — glutamate inhibitor, prolongs survival by ~3 months.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Edaravone","detail":"Reduces functional decline in early ALS with good lung function (ALSFRS-R selection criteria)."},{"naam":"Nutrition","detail":"PEG tube for dysphagia — prevents aspiration pneumonia and malnutrition."},{"naam":"Respiration","detail":"NIV (non-invasive ventilation) if FVC <50% or symptomatic hypercapnia — prolongs life and reduces symptoms."},{"naam":"MDT care","detail":"Multidisciplinary: neurologist, rehabilitation physician, speech therapist, dietitian, psychologist — different needs at every stage."}]},{"_i":15,"q":"Carbamazepine is contraindicated in carriers of the HLA-B*1502 allele because of a strongly increased risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. HLA-B*1502 (prevalent in Southeast Asian populations) is strongly associated with carbamazepine-induced SJS/TEN. Genetic screening before starting is recommended. Alternative: levetiracetam or lamotrigine.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"HLA-B*1502 is a genetic variant that confers a strongly increased risk of Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) when using aromatic antiepileptic drugs, especially carbamazepine. Prevalence: 5-15% in Southeast Asian populations.","wiki_redflag":"SJS/TEN is life-threatening — TEN mortality up to 30%. With rash + mucosal lesions during carbamazepine: stop immediately and consult dermatology.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"HLA-B*1502 and carbamazepine","desc":"HLA-B*1502 presents carbamazepine metabolites as "foreign" to T cells → massive cytotoxic T-cell response → keratinocyte apoptosis → skin blistering."},{"title":"Aromatic antiepileptic drugs","desc":"Carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine and oxcarbazepine carry this risk. Levetiracetam and valproate are HLA-independent."},{"title":"Screening","desc":"Before starting carbamazepine in patients of Asian origin: HLA-B*1502 genotyping. If positive: choose an alternative."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Carbamazepine + HLA-B*1502","desc":"High SJS/TEN risk. Screening required in Southeast Asian patients. Alternative: levetiracetam."},{"label":"Lamotrigine","desc":"Rash in 10% (dose-dependent) but lower SJS risk. Slow titration mandatory."},{"label":"Levetiracetam","desc":"No SJS risk, no HLA interaction. First choice in patients with HLA-B*1502."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop carbamazepine immediately with skin reaction + mucosal lesions.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Substitute","detail":"Switch to levetiracetam or valproate — not an aromatic antiepileptic drug."},{"naam":"SJS treatment","detail":"ICU admission, wound care (burn center), consider IVIG or ciclosporin."},{"naam":"Screening","detail":"HLA-B*1502 genotyping before starting in Asian patients — now recommended in guidelines."}]},{"_i":16,"q":"This drug blocks H⁺/K⁺-ATPase on the parietal cell and inhibits acid production. Used in GERD and peptic ulcers. Which drug is this?","a":["Omeprazole","Metoclopramide","Famotidine","Sucralfate"],"ex":"Omeprazole is a proton pump inhibitor (PPI). Famotidine is an H2 blocker — less effective, different target.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) are the most potent gastric acid suppressants. They irreversibly block H⁺/K⁺-ATPase (proton pump) on the parietal cell → acid production falls by >90%. Take 30 min before breakfast — parietal cells must be active for activation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Prodrug activation","desc":"PPIs are prodrugs that are converted into the active sulfenamide in the acidic environment of the parietal cell."},{"title":"Irreversible binding","desc":"Sulfenamide binds covalently to H⁺/K⁺-ATPase → effect lasts 24-48 h despite a short half-life in blood."},{"title":"Daily intake","desc":"New proton pumps are continuously produced → PPI must be taken daily to maintain effect."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Proton pump inhibitors (PPI)","desc":"Irreversible proton pump blockade, >90% acid reduction, preferred drug for GERD/PU. Take 30 min before breakfast."},{"label":"H2 blockers (famotidine)","desc":"Less effective, rapid tachyphylaxis. Useful for nocturnal gastric symptoms or if PPI is not tolerated."},{"label":"Antacids (aluminum hydroxide)","desc":"Immediate but short-lived acid neutralization, no ulcer healing — symptomatic treatment only."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Indications: GERD, peptic ulcer, H. pylori eradication (triple therapy), NSAID protection in high risk, Zollinger-Ellison.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Adverse effects of long-term use","detail":"increased risk of C. diff, fractures (reduced Ca absorption), magnesium deficiency, vitamin B12 deficiency, interstitial nephritis."}]},{"_i":17,"q":"Beta-1-selective drug, lowers HR and BP, used in hypertension and heart failure. Known caveat: asthma. Which drug is this?","a":["Amlodipine","Metoprolol","Spironolactone","Hydralazine"],"ex":"Metoprolol is a cardioselective beta-1 blocker. Beta-2 blockade causes bronchospasm — dangerous in asthma.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"Beta-blockers block beta-adrenergic receptors. Beta-1-selective agents (metoprolol, bisoprolol) are cardioselective: lower heart rate + contractility + AV conduction, with minimal beta-2 adverse effects.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Beta-1 blockade","desc":"Negative chronotropic (lower HR) + negative inotropic (less contractile force) + negative dromotropic (slower AV conduction)."},{"title":"Oxygen consumption ↓","desc":"Lower heart rate and contractility reduce myocardial oxygen consumption — effective in angina and post-MI."},{"title":"In heart failure","desc":"Paradoxically effective despite negative inotropy — neurohumoral inhibition improves long-term remodeling. Start low, titrate slowly."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Beta-blockers (metoprolol)","desc":"Beta-1-selective, negative chronotropic/inotropic. Caveat: asthma, AV block, PAD. Never stop abruptly."},{"label":"Non-DHP calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem)","desc":"Also negative chronotropic but via L-type Ca²⁺ channel inhibition — do not combine with beta-blocker."},{"label":"Amlodipine (dihydropyridine)","desc":"Only vasodilating, no heart rate effect — suitable in heart failure + angina."},{"label":"Ivabradine","desc":"Inhibits only the sinus node (If current) without negative inotropy — useful in beta-blocker intolerance or as add-on."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Indications: hypertension, angina, HFrEF, AF rate control, post-MI.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Contraindications","detail":"Severe asthma, symptomatic bradycardia, AV block °II-III, acute decompensated heart failure."},{"naam":"Tapering","detail":"Never stop abruptly — rebound: tachycardia, angina, hypertension. Taper over 2-4 weeks."}]},{"_i":18,"q":"This drug inhibits ACE, lowers angiotensin II and aldosterone. Known adverse effect: dry cough. Which drug is this?","a":["Losartan","Lisinopril","Verapamil","Furosemide"],"ex":"Lisinopril = ACE inhibitor. Losartan is an ARB (angiotensin II receptor blocker) — no cough as adverse effect.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"ACE inhibitors (lisinopril, enalapril, ramipril) block angiotensin-converting enzyme → less angiotensin II → vasodilation + less aldosterone → blood pressure reduction. They provide renal protection in diabetes and reduce mortality in HFrEF.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"ACE inhibition","desc":"ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II and breaks down bradykinin. Blockade lowers angiotensin II (less vasoconstriction, less aldosterone) and causes bradykinin accumulation."},{"title":"Bradykinin → cough","desc":"Bradykinin accumulation stimulates prostaglandin synthesis and irritates cough receptors in the throat → dry cough in 10-15%. This is a class adverse effect, not an allergy."},{"title":"ARBs vs ACE inhibitors","desc":"ARBs (losartan, valsartan) directly block the AT1 receptor — same antihypertensive effect, same nephroprotection, without bradykinin accumulation → no cough."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ACE inhibitors (lisinopril)","desc":"Lower angiotensin II + accumulate bradykinin. Caveat: cough (10-15%), angioedema, hyperkalemia, teratogenic."},{"label":"ARB (losartan, valsartan)","desc":"Same indications as ACE inhibitor, no cough. Choice in ACE inhibitor intolerance."},{"label":"ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)","desc":"ARB + neprilysin inhibitor — superior to ACE inhibitor in HFrEF (PARADIGM-HF). Replaces ACE inhibitor, never combine."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Indications: hypertension, HFrEF, nephroprotection in diabetic nephropathy, post-MI.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Contraindications","detail":"Pregnancy (teratogenic!), bilateral renal artery stenosis, hyperkalemia, prior angioedema with ACE inhibitor."},{"naam":"Dose escalation","detail":"Start low (e.g. lisinopril 2.5 mg), increase guided by blood pressure and creatinine/K⁺."}]},{"_i":19,"q":"Antibiotic that inhibits DNA gyrase, active against gram-negative bacteria. Caveat: tendinitis. Which antibiotic is this?","a":["Amoxicillin","Doxycycline","Ciprofloxacin","Metronidazole"],"ex":"Ciprofloxacin is a fluoroquinolone. Adverse effects: tendinitis, QTc prolongation. Caveat in older adults + corticosteroids.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV — enzymes that unwind DNA for replication. Broad gram-negative spectrum. Bactericidal. Three class adverse effects to remember: tendon, QTc, C. diff.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Tendinitis & tendon rupture","desc":"Disruption of collagen synthesis in tendon tissue, especially Achilles tendon. Risk increased in older adults, corticosteroids and renal insufficiency."},{"title":"QTc prolongation","desc":"hERG channel inhibition → prolonged repolarization → torsade de pointes risk when combined with other QTc-prolonging drugs (antipsychotics, antiarrhythmics)."},{"title":"C. diff risk","desc":"Broad spectrum disrupts gut flora — fluoroquinolones are an important trigger for Clostridioides difficile infection."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin)","desc":"DNA gyrase inhibitor. Broad gram-negative + atypical spectrum. Caveat: tendon, QTc, C. diff, increasing resistance."},{"label":"Beta-lactams (amoxicillin, cephalosporins)","desc":"Cell wall inhibition via PBP. No tendinitis, no DNA damage. First choice for most infections."},{"label":"Doxycycline","desc":"30S ribosome inhibitor. Also effective against atypical pathogens (Chlamydia, Mycoplasma). No fluoroquinolone adverse effects."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":20,"q":"Which anticoagulant inhibits vitamin K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX and X?","a":["Heparin","Warfarin","Rivaroxaban","Dabigatran"],"ex":"Warfarin/acenocoumarol inhibit the vitamin K cycle → reduced production of clotting factors. Monitor INR.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"VKAs (warfarin, acenocoumarol) inhibit vitamin K-dependent clotting factors II, VII, IX, X and anticoagulant proteins C and S. Effect begins only after 3-5 days — existing factors must first be degraded. Narrow therapeutic range: INR 2-3.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"VKOR inhibition","desc":"Vitamin K is required for gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors. VKAs block vitamin K epoxide reductase (VKOR) → oxidized vitamin K cannot be regenerated."},{"title":"Inactive clotting factors","desc":"Without active vitamin K, clotting proteins (II, VII, IX, X) are produced as inactive precursors. Factor VII has the shortest half-life → INR rises fastest."},{"title":"INR monitoring","desc":"INR measures delay in the extrinsic coagulation pathway. Therapeutic INR: 2-3 (most indications), 2.5-3.5 with mechanical heart valve."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"VKA (warfarin/acenocoumarol)","desc":"Indirect via VKOR inhibition. INR monitoring required. Choice for prosthetic valves, mitral stenosis. Numerous interactions (CYP2C9)."},{"label":"Heparin (LMWH/UFH)","desc":"Direct via antithrombin III, parenteral. Immediate effect — for bridging, acute situations and during pregnancy."},{"label":"DOACs (rivaroxaban, apixaban, dabigatran)","desc":"Direct acting, no INR monitoring. Contraindicated in mechanical prosthetic valves and severe mitral stenosis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Therapeutic INR: 2-3 (most indications), 2.5-3.5 (mechanical heart valve).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"INR too high (bleeding)","detail":"Vitamin K po/IV + 4-factor PCC for severe/life-threatening bleeding."},{"naam":"INR too low","detail":"Increase dose, always check CYP2C9 interactions with new medication."},{"naam":"CYP2C9 interactions","detail":"Many drugs increase (antibiotics, azoles) or decrease (rifampicin, St John's wort) INR — always check with new medication."}]},{"_i":21,"q":"Metformin is contraindicated at eGFR < 30 ml/min because of the risk of lactic acidosis.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Metformin accumulates in renal insufficiency. eGFR 30–45: halve the dose. eGFR < 30: stop permanently.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":22,"q":"Heparin has a direct anticoagulant effect and can be administered orally.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Heparin works only IV or SC (molecule too large for oral absorption). Effect is immediate via antithrombin III.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":23,"q":"Patient with epilepsy and bipolar disorder. Which antiepileptic drug also has a proven mood-stabilizing indication?","a":["Levetiracetam","Valproate (sodium valproate)","Gabapentin","Phenobarbital"],"ex":"Valproate: broad-spectrum antiepileptic drug and mood stabilizer in bipolar disorder. CAVEAT: severe teratogenicity (neural tube defects, cognitive effects in child) — absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy wish without adequate contraception.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":24,"q":"Statins lower only LDL cholesterol and have no effect on triglycerides or HDL.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Statins also moderately lower triglycerides (10-30%) and slightly increase HDL (5-10%). The primary effect is LDL reduction (20-60% depending on statin and dose), but the full lipid profile improves.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":25,"q":"HIV patient starts tenofovir/emtricitabine + efavirenz. After 3 weeks: vivid dreams, depressed mood, concentration problems. Most likely cause?","a":["Tenofovir nephrotoxicity","Efavirenz CNS adverse effects","Immune reconstitution syndrome (IRIS)","HIV encephalopathy"],"ex":"Efavirenz (NNRTI): known CNS adverse effects in the first weeks — vivid dreams, nightmares, depression, dizziness. Taking it at bedtime reduces inconvenience. Spontaneous improvement after 2-4 weeks in most patients.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":26,"q":"Which mechanism explains the efficacy of methotrexate in rheumatoid arthritis?","a":["COX-2 inhibition","TNF-α blockade","DHFR inhibition → anti-inflammatory via adenosine","IL-6 receptor blockade"],"ex":"Methotrexate inhibits DHFR (dihydrofolate reductase) → reduced purine synthesis → adenosine accumulation → anti-inflammatory effect. Folic acid supplementation reduces adverse effects (mucositis, myelosuppression) without loss of efficacy.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":"In RA, methotrexate does NOT primarily work through the folate pathway (as in cancer). At low dose, adenosine accumulates via AICAR → A2A receptor activation → anti-inflammatory. MTX is the cornerstone of RA treatment; most biologics are added to it.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"High dose (oncology)","desc":"DHFR inhibition → no purine synthesis → no DNA replication → cell growth stopped."},{"title":"Low dose (RA)","desc":"AICAR accumulation → adenosine accumulation → A2A receptor activation → inhibition of T-cell activation and pro-inflammatory cytokine production."},{"title":"Folic acid interaction","desc":"Folic acid supplementation (5 mg/week) reduces adverse effects (mucositis, myelosuppression, liver fibrosis) without loss of efficacy."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Methotrexate (MTX)","desc":"Anchor DMARD in RA. Effect after 6-12 weeks. Caveat: liver, myelosuppression, pulmonary fibrosis, teratogenic. Always add folic acid."},{"label":"TNF-α blockers (adalimumab, etanercept)","desc":"Biologics — faster acting, second line after MTX failure or combined with MTX."},{"label":"IL-6 inhibitor (tocilizumab)","desc":"Effective in MTX failure or if MTX is not tolerated. No TNF-α pathway."},{"label":"Hydroxychloroquine","desc":"Mild DMARD for early or seronegative RA. Less toxic but also less potent than MTX."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":27,"q":"Aminoglycosides (e.g. gentamicin) are concentration-dependent bactericidal. Which dosing schedule optimally uses this?","a":["Continuous infusion for stable level","Once-daily high dose","Four times daily low dose","Oral administration for better absorption"],"ex":"Once-daily aminoglycosides: high Cmax/MIC ratio → maximal bactericidal activity + post-antibiotic effect. Lower nephrotoxicity due to recovery time (tubular cells). Oral absorption of aminoglycosides is negligible.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":28,"q":"Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) significantly increase the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. PPIs increase gastric pH → reduced acid barrier → colonization by C. difficile. Meta-analyses: OR ~1.7 for CDI with PPI use. Additional risks of long-term use: hypomagnesemia, pneumonia, osteoporosis.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":29,"q":"22-year-old young man: fever, extreme fatigue, pharyngitis, cervical lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly. Monospot positive. Which diagnosis fits best?","a":["Streptococcal tonsillitis","CMV infection","EBV mononucleosis","Primary HIV infection"],"ex":"EBV mononucleosis: fever + sore throat + extreme fatigue + splenomegaly. Crucial: do NOT give amoxicillin — 80% develop a generalized rash (not a true penicillin allergy!). Avoid contact sports for 4 weeks (risk of splenic rupture).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"EBV infects B lymphocytes via the CD21 receptor. The symptoms (fever, extreme fatigue, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy) are NOT caused by the virus itself but by the massive CD8+ T-cell response trying to eliminate infected B cells.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Immune response","desc":"Symptoms are caused by the massive CD8+ T-cell response that eliminates infected B cells — not by the virus itself."},{"title":"Splenomegaly","desc":"Lymphocytic infiltration of the spleen → splenomegaly in 50-80% → risk of splenic rupture with contact sports or trauma (avoid for 4 weeks)."},{"title":"Diagnostics","desc":"Monospot detects heterophile IgM antibodies (cross-reaction with sheep erythrocytes). Caveat: false negative in the first week and in young children."},{"title":"Caveat amoxicillin","desc":"Amoxicillin in active EBV causes a generalized rash in ~80% — immune-mediated, not a true penicillin allergy!"}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"EBV mononucleosis","desc":"Monospot positive, extreme fatigue, pharyngitis, splenomegaly. Do not give amoxicillin! Avoid contact sports for 4 weeks."},{"label":"CMV mononucleosis","desc":"Monospot negative, milder throat symptoms, more liver dysfunction. CMV PCR/antibodies diagnostic."},{"label":"Primary HIV infection","desc":"Also mononucleosis-like + rash, high viral HIV RNA, monospot negative — always consider."},{"label":"Streptococcal tonsillitis","desc":"No splenomegaly, monospot negative, GABHS culture positive — antibiotics are indicated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":30,"q":"Returning traveler from Asia: cyclic fever every 48 hours, chills, sweats. Thick blood smear positive. Which diagnosis fits best?","a":["Typhoid fever","Malaria","Dengue","Leishmaniasis"],"ex":"Malaria: cyclic fever after tropical travel → always rule out, even with prophylaxis use. P. falciparum lacks the fixed cycle but is the deadliest (cerebral malaria, ARDS). Thick blood smear is the gold standard. Treatment: artemisinin combination therapy.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites, transmitted by the Anopheles mosquito. Five species: P. falciparum (deadliest), P. vivax, P. ovale, P. malariae, P. knowlesi.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Mosquito bite","desc":"Anopheles mosquito injects sporozoites into the host's bloodstream."},{"title":"Liver phase","desc":"Sporozoites reach the liver → asymptomatic replication in hepatocytes (1-2 weeks, exo-erythrocytic phase)."},{"title":"Bloodstream","desc":"Merozoites released from hepatocytes → infect red blood cells (erythrocytic phase → fever and symptoms)."},{"title":"Cyclic rupture","desc":"Synchronized rupture of infected erythrocytes → fever spike. P. falciparum: sticky cells → microangiopathy and cerebral malaria."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Malaria","desc":"Cyclic fever, chills, sweats after tropical travel. Thick blood smear is the gold standard. P. falciparum deadliest."},{"label":"Dengue","desc":"Severe muscle/joint pain ('breakbone fever'), rash, low platelets, no cyclic pattern. NS1 antigen diagnostic."},{"label":"Typhoid","desc":"Persistent fever (no cyclic pattern), abdominal pain, relative bradycardia. Blood culture diagnostic."},{"label":"Leptospirosis","desc":"Water exposure, conjunctival suffusion, muscle symptoms — Weil disease in severe form."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"P. falciparum: artemisinin combination therapy (e.g. artemether-lumefantrine).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Severe malaria","detail":"IV artesunate for decreased consciousness, severe anemia or organ failure."},{"naam":"P. vivax/ovale","detail":"Chloroquine + primaquine to eliminate liver forms (caveat G6PD deficiency before starting)."},{"naam":"Prophylaxis","detail":"Standard prophylaxis depending on destination and resistance pattern: atovaquone-proguanil, mefloquine or doxycycline."}]},{"_i":31,"q":"Amoxicillin is the first choice for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a young woman in the Netherlands.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Nitrofurantoin or fosfomycin are first choice because of high resistance to amoxicillin in E. coli.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":32,"q":"72-year-old man, 2 h after successful PCI for inferior STEMI: sudden bradycardia 38/min, hypotension 80/50, elevated JVP, clear lungs. Most likely cause?","a":["Cardiogenic shock","Right ventricular infarction","Papillary muscle rupture","Ventricular septal rupture"],"ex":"RV infarction in inferior MI (RCA occlusion): triad of hypotension + high JVP + clear lungs. CAVEAT: nitrates and diuretics are contraindicated — preload is essential! Volume loading is the first step.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Right ventricular infarction (RVI) occurs in 30–50% of inferior MIs due to proximal RCA occlusion. The RV fails as a pump → high JVP but clear lungs — the classic pitfall that forbids nitrates and diuretics.","wiki_redflag":"Nitrates and diuretics are absolutely contraindicated in RVI — they reduce preload and immediately worsen shock.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"RCA occlusion","desc":"The proximal RCA supplies the RV myocardium — occlusion causes immediate RV dysfunction."},{"title":"RV pump failure","desc":"RV contracts insufficiently → back-pressure → high JVP, distended neck veins."},{"title":"LV underfilling","desc":"RV pumps too little blood to LV (preload dependence) → low cardiac output → hypotension."},{"title":"Clear lungs","desc":"The LV itself is intact — no pulmonary edema. Combination of hypotension + high JVP + clear lungs = RVI until proven otherwise."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"RV infarction","desc":"Hypotension + elevated JVP + clear lungs after inferior STEMI. V4R: ST elevation."},{"label":"LV infarction/cardiogenic shock","desc":"Hypotension but also pulmonary edema and crackles — LV is the problem."},{"label":"Cardiac tamponade","desc":"Also hypotension + high JVP but muffled heart sounds, echo proves it. No MI ECG."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"High JVP but pleuritic pain, no ST elevation in V4R, D-dimer elevated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Volume loading: NaCl 0.9% IV 500 ml bolus — the RV needs preload to pump.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Urgent PCI","detail":"Recanalization of the RCA is the definitive treatment — the faster, the better RV recovery."},{"naam":"Noradrenaline","detail":"For refractory shock after volume loading — maintains coronary perfusion pressure."},{"naam":"ECG diagnostics","detail":"Always obtain V4R in inferior MI — ST elevation ≥1 mm confirms RVI."}]},{"_i":33,"q":"55-year-old woman, exertional dyspnea, ECHO: EF 65%, elevated filling pressures, no valve abnormalities. Diagnosis?","a":["Heart failure with reduced EF (HFrEF)","Heart failure with preserved EF (HFpEF)","Pulmonary hypertension","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"ex":"HFpEF: the heart pumps well (EF ≥50%) but fills poorly — diastolic dysfunction. Distinguish from HFrEF only by echo. SGLT2 inhibitors (dapagliflozin) recently proven effective; ACE inhibitors/beta-blockers do not reduce mortality here.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"HFpEF: the heart pumps normally (EF ≥50%) but fills poorly due to a stiff, non-relaxing LV. The problem is in diastole, not systole.","wiki_redflag":"ACE inhibitors and beta-blockers reduce mortality in HFrEF but NOT in HFpEF — the distinction is clinically crucial.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Diastolic dysfunction","desc":"LV relaxation is delayed → filling requires more pressure → elevated E/e' on echo (>14 = diagnostic)."},{"title":"LV hypertrophy","desc":"Chronic hypertension → concentric hypertrophy → stiffer ventricular wall."},{"title":"Elevated filling pressure","desc":"High LVEDP backs up to the left atrium and lungs → exertional dyspnea, orthopnea."},{"title":"Preserved EF","desc":"Contraction intact — EF ≥50%. Distinguish from HFrEF only by echocardiography."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"HFpEF (EF ≥50%)","desc":"Diastolic dysfunction, stiff LV, normal contraction. SGLT2 inhibitors show benefit."},{"label":"HFrEF (EF <40%)","desc":"Systolic dysfunction — proven mortality reduction with ACE-i + beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i."},{"label":"Pulmonary hypertension","desc":"RV overload, P pulmonale on ECG, no LV hypertrophy. Echo distinguishes."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Diuretics for symptom relief (edema, dyspnea) — relieve symptoms but do not prolong life.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"SGLT2 inhibitor","detail":"Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin — the first proven drugs with mortality benefit in HFpEF."},{"naam":"Risk factors","detail":"Aggressively treat hypertension, diabetes and obesity — these are the causes of HFpEF."},{"naam":"No beta-blocker/ACE-i","detail":"No proven mortality reduction in HFpEF — do not add routinely."}]},{"_i":34,"q":"26-year-old man, athlete: syncope during exertion, positive family history of sudden cardiac death. ECHO: asymmetric septal hypertrophy 22 mm, SAM of mitral valve. Diagnosis?","a":["Aortic valve stenosis","Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy (HOCM)","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Pulmonary embolism"],"ex":"HOCM: septal hypertrophy + SAM (systolic anterior motion) of mitral valve → dynamic LVOT obstruction. Most important cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes. ICD indication in high-risk profile. Sports restriction mandatory.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"HOCM is an autosomal-dominant sarcomere mutation that causes asymmetric septal hypertrophy. It is the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young people and athletes.","wiki_redflag":"The murmur of HOCM increases with Valsalva (less filling → more obstruction) — this is the opposite of aortic stenosis.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Septal hypertrophy","desc":"Thickened IVS obstructs the LVOT during systole → outflow gradient."},{"title":"SAM (systolic anterior motion)","desc":"Mitral valve moves anteriorly due to Venturi effect → worsens LVOT obstruction + mitral regurgitation."},{"title":"Dynamic obstruction","desc":"Increases with ↓ preload (standing, Valsalva, dehydration) and ↓ afterload; decreases with ↑ preload (squatting)."},{"title":"Arrhythmias","desc":"Hypertrophic myocardium is arrhythmogenic → VT/VF → syncope or sudden cardiac death, also in young athletes."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"HOCM","desc":"Murmur increases with Valsalva/standing; asymmetric septal hypertrophy on echo; family history."},{"label":"Aortic stenosis","desc":"Murmur decreases with Valsalva (fixed mechanical obstruction). Calcification on echo."},{"label":"WPW syndrome","desc":"Also syncope in young people but delta wave and short PR on ECG — no echo abnormality."},{"label":"Dilated CMP","desc":"Dilated LV with low EF — opposite of HOCM."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Beta-blocker or verapamil — lower heart rate → longer filling time → less LVOT gradient.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ICD indication","detail":"With ≥1 risk factor: family member with sudden cardiac death, unexplained syncope, NSVT, septum ≥30 mm, hypotension with exertion."},{"naam":"Sports restriction","detail":"Competitive sports prohibited — exertion increases LVOT gradient and arrhythmia risk."},{"naam":"Septal reduction","detail":"Surgical myectomy or alcohol ablation for refractory LVOT obstruction (gradient >50 mmHg)."}]},{"_i":35,"q":"Hypokalemia (K⁺ < 3.5) is an independent risk factor for ventricular fibrillation in acute myocardial infarction.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Hypokalemia lowers the threshold for ventricular arrhythmias, especially in ischemia. Target K⁺ ≥ 4.0 mmol/L in ACS is recommended. Active supplementation is part of standard care in the CCU.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Potassium is crucial for the resting membrane potential of cardiomyocytes. Hypokalemia increases automaticity and prolongs repolarization → arrhythmia threshold lowered, especially in ischemic myocardium.","wiki_redflag":"In the CCU: K⁺ ≥4.0 mmol/L and Mg²⁺ ≥0.8 mmol/L as targets — below this doubles arrhythmia risk in ACS.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Resting membrane potential","desc":"Lower extracellular K⁺ → larger K⁺ gradient → membrane potential more negative → cell is hyperexcitable."},{"title":"Prolonged repolarization","desc":"K⁺ channels work less effectively → slower repolarization → QTc prolongation → risk of torsades de pointes."},{"title":"Ischemic synergism","desc":"Ischemic myocardium already loses K⁺ via ATP-sensitive K⁺ channels → combination with hypokalemia gives a dangerously low threshold for VF."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hypokalemia (K⁺ <3.5)","desc":"Flattened T wave, prominent U wave, prolonged QTc → risk of torsades. Causes: diuretics, vomiting, diarrhea, hyperaldosteronism."},{"label":"Hyperkalemia (K⁺ >5.5)","desc":"Peaked T waves, wide QRS, sine-wave pattern at K⁺ >7 → VF risk by another route. Treat with calcium, glucose+insulin, bicarbonate."},{"label":"Hypomagnesemia","desc":"Aggravates hypokalemia (Mg²⁺ deficiency makes K⁺ supplementation refractory) — always supplement together."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Oral/IV K⁺ supplementation for K⁺ <3.5. In CCU: IV KCl if K⁺ <3.0 or ECG abnormalities.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"CCU target","detail":"K⁺ ≥4.0 mmol/L + Mg²⁺ ≥0.8 mmol/L — check 1-2× per day in ACS patients."},{"naam":"Maximum infusion rate","detail":"Peripheral: max 10-20 mmol/h. Central: max 40 mmol/h with ECG monitoring."}]},{"_i":36,"q":"Patient with heart failure EF 30%, intolerance to ACE inhibitor due to angioedema. Best alternative?","a":["Verapamil","ARB (e.g. valsartan)","High-dose amlodipine","Diltiazem"],"ex":"ARB does not cause angioedema — bradykinin pathway not involved. Verapamil and diltiazem are negative inotropic and contraindicated in low EF. Hydralazine + nitrate is second choice in ARB intolerance.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Angioedema with ACE inhibitors is a class effect via bradykinin accumulation — not an allergic mechanism. ARBs directly block the AT1 receptor and do not affect bradykinin → no angioedema.","wiki_redflag":"Verapamil and diltiazem are absolutely contraindicated in HFrEF — the negative inotropic effect further lowers the already low EF and worsens heart failure.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"ACE inhibitor angioedema","desc":"ACE breaks down bradykinin. Inhibition → bradykinin accumulates → activation of bradykinin B2 receptors → vasodilation and edema of skin/mucosa. Not IgE-mediated — antihistamines do not help."},{"title":"ARB mechanism","desc":"ARBs directly block the angiotensin II type-1 receptor (AT1). ACE is not inhibited → bradykinin level normal → no angioedema (incidence <0.1% vs 0.1-0.7% with ACE-i)."},{"title":"Alternative in ARB intolerance","desc":"Hydralazine (arterial vasodilator) + isosorbide dinitrate (venous) — proven alternative in African Americans and in both ACE-i and ARB intolerance (A-HeFT trial)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ARB (valsartan, candesartan)","desc":"Same RAAS blockade as ACE inhibitor, without bradykinin effect. Equal nephroprotection and mortality reduction in HFrEF. First choice in ACE inhibitor intolerance."},{"label":"ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)","desc":"Superior to ACE inhibitor/ARB in stable HFrEF (PARADIGM-HF) — but with history of angioedema: use caution (sacubitril increases bradykinin)."},{"label":"Verapamil/diltiazem","desc":"Non-DHP calcium channel blockers: negative inotropic and chronotropic → contraindicated in HFrEF. Only exception: amlodipine (DHP, no negative inotropic effect) is safe."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop ACE inhibitor immediately in angioedema. Replace with ARB after recovery (wait at least 4-6 weeks).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ARB dosing","detail":"Start low, titrate upward: valsartan 40 mg bid → target 160 mg bid (CHARM trial dose)."},{"naam":"Consider ARNI","detail":"Sacubitril/valsartan as next step in stable patient after at least 36 h ACE-inhibitor-free period."}]},{"_i":37,"q":"First-degree AV block (PR interval > 200 ms) in an asymptomatic patient always requires a pacemaker.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. First-degree AV block in an asymptomatic patient: no treatment needed, observation only. Pacemaker is indicated for symptomatic second-degree Mobitz II or third-degree AV block.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"First-degree AV block = PR >200 ms but every P wave conducts to a QRS. It is a delay in the AV node, not a block — all atrial impulses reach the ventricles.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Delayed AV node conduction","desc":"Every atrial impulse reaches the ventricles but more slowly. Causes: vagal hypertonia (athletes), inferior wall ischemia, digoxin, beta-blocker, calcium channel blocker."},{"title":"No hemodynamic consequence","desc":"All impulses reach the ventricles → normal heart rate and cardiac output → asymptomatic. Observation only."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"1st-degree AV block","desc":"PR >200 ms, every P→QRS. Asymptomatic, no treatment. Can progress to 2nd-degree with certain causes."},{"label":"2nd-degree Mobitz II","desc":"Constant PR but sudden QRS drop — His bundle damage → risk of complete block → urgent pacemaker evaluation."},{"label":"3rd-degree (complete) AV block","desc":"P and QRS completely dissociated — always urgent pacemaker needed, regardless of symptoms."},{"label":"Wenckebach (Mobitz I)","desc":"Progressively longer PR until QRS drop → reset. Benign, AV node level, no pacemaker needed unless symptomatic."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"First-degree AV block: no treatment. Look for underlying cause (medication, ischemia).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stop culprit medication","detail":"Digoxin, beta-blocker or non-DHP calcium channel blocker as cause → stop or reduce dose."},{"naam":"Follow-up","detail":"Annual check for persistent 1st-degree block — progression to higher grade is rare but possible."}]},{"_i":38,"q":"HIV patient, CD4 45/µL:\nFever, dry cough, exertional dyspnea\nLDH 520 U/L\nBilateral interstitial infiltrates\nMost likely pathogen?","a":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Pneumocystis jirovecii (PCP)","CMV pneumonitis","Aspergillus fumigatus"],"ex":"PCP at CD4 <200/µL: insidious presentation, dry cough, high LDH, bilateral "ground glass" infiltrates. Treatment: high-dose TMP-SMX (3 weeks). Prophylaxis mandatory at CD4 <200. Add corticosteroids in severe hypoxemia.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":39,"q":"Patient: HBsAg positive, anti-HBc IgM positive, anti-HBs negative, HBeAg positive. Interpretation?","a":["Chronic HBV infection","Acute hepatitis B, highly infectious","Recovery from HBV (immunity)","Vaccination immunity"],"ex":"Acute HBV: HBsAg+ and anti-HBc IgM+ (recent infection). HBeAg+ indicates active viral replication, highly infectious. Anti-HBs positive = recovery or vaccination. Chronic HBV: HBsAg >6 months, IgM anti-HBc negative.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Reading HBV serology: HBsAg = virus present. Anti-HBc IgM = recent infection.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Early infection","desc":"After infection, HBsAg appears as the first marker (after 2-10 weeks). IgM anti-HBc follows — proves a recent infection."},{"title":"Active replication","desc":"HBeAg positivity indicates high viral load and high infectivity risk. Symptoms: jaundice, fatigue, anorexia."},{"title":"Recovery & chronicity","desc":"Recovery: HBsAg disappears → anti-HBs appears (window period: both negative). Chronic: HBsAg >6 months, IgM anti-HBc gone. Vaccination: only anti-HBs+, never anti-HBc."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Acute HBV (highly infectious)","desc":"HBsAg+ + anti-HBc IgM+ + HBeAg+ = recent infection with active replication. High transmission risk."},{"label":"Chronic HBV","desc":"HBsAg >6 months, anti-HBc IgG (no IgM) — check ALT + HBV DNA to determine treatment need."},{"label":"Immunotolerant","desc":"High HBV DNA, normal ALT — no treatment yet but follow-up needed."},{"label":"Active chronic hepatitis","desc":"Elevated ALT + high HBV DNA → antiviral therapy (tenofovir or entecavir)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":40,"q":"MRSA resistance is based on the mecA gene, which encodes PBP2a — a transpeptidase with low affinity for all beta-lactam antibiotics.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. PBP2a does not bind beta-lactams effectively → cell wall synthesis continues → resistance. Treatment: vancomycin, linezolid or daptomycin. MRSA diagnostics by PCR (mecA) or phenotypic susceptibility testing.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":41,"q":"40-year-old man, sub-Saharan Africa: malaria despite chloroquine prophylaxis. Most resistant Plasmodium species?","a":["P. vivax","P. falciparum","P. ovale","P. malariae"],"ex":"P. falciparum: responsible for >90% of malaria mortality. Widespread chloroquine resistance in sub-Saharan Africa. Correct prophylaxis: atovaquone-proguanil or mefloquine. Falciparum can cause cerebral malaria — treat quickly.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":42,"q":"After 7 days of amoxicillin-clavulanate: watery diarrhea 8×/day, fever, abdominal cramps, leukocytes in stool. Toxin test positive. Diagnosis?","a":["Salmonella enteritis","Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI)","Viral gastroenteritis","Campylobacter jejuni"],"ex":"CDI: typical after (broad-spectrum) antibiotics. Toxin A/B diagnostic. First episode: oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin (metronidazole no longer first choice). CAVEAT: no loperamide — risk of toxic megacolon.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":"Clostridioides difficile (C. diff) is a spore-forming bacterium that colonizes the colon when normal gut flora is disrupted — classically after antibiotics. Toxins A and B damage the colonic epithelium → pseudomembranous colitis with watery diarrhea, cramps, fever.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Flora disruption","desc":"Antibiotics (especially clindamycin, cephalosporins, fluoroquinolones) clear commensal flora. C. diff spores survive and germinate."},{"title":"Toxin production","desc":"Vegetative C. diff forms produce toxins A and B — damage colonic epithelium and induce an inflammatory response."},{"title":"Pseudomembranous colitis","desc":"Epithelial damage → watery diarrhea, cramps, fever, leukocytosis. Severe cases: toxic megacolon."},{"title":"Infection control","desc":"Spores are resistant to alcohol hand sanitizer → soap and water mandatory with CDI patients!"}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Clostridioides difficile infection (CDI)","desc":"After antibiotics: watery diarrhea + fever + leukocytes in stool. Toxin A/B positive. Treatment: vancomycin or fidaxomicin."},{"label":"Non-CDI antibiotic diarrhea","desc":"No toxins present, less severe, stops when antibiotic is stopped."},{"label":"IBD exacerbation","desc":"No clear antibiotic history, chronic course, colonoscopy diagnostic."},{"label":"Ischemic colitis","desc":"Older adults, sudden onset, blood in stool, vascular risk factors — no antibiotic history."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop the triggering antibiotic immediately.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"First episode","detail":"Vancomycin 125 mg 4x/day PO 10 days OR fidaxomicin — both superior to metronidazole."},{"naam":"No metronidazole","detail":"No longer first choice for CDI — vancomycin or fidaxomicin are more effective."},{"naam":"No loperamide","detail":"Contraindicated in CDI — suppresses bowel motility → risk of toxic megacolon."},{"naam":"Recurrence","detail":"Extended fidaxomicin regimen or fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT) for multiple recurrences."}]},{"_i":43,"q":"Teenager with mononucleosis-like presentation receives amoxicillin. Next day: extensive maculopapular exanthem over the whole body. Meaning?","a":["Proven penicillin allergy","Amoxicillin exanthem in EBV — not a true allergy","Serum sickness","Stevens-Johnson syndrome"],"ex":"Amoxicillin in active EBV: ~80% chance of generalized exanthem — immune-mediated, NOT an IgE allergy. Patient does not need to avoid penicillin in the future. Document correctly to avoid an incorrect allergy label.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":44,"q":"Rifampicin is a potent CYP3A4 inducer and can significantly reduce the efficacy of oral contraceptives.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Rifampicin strongly induces CYP3A4 → accelerated metabolism of ethinylestradiol → reduced contraceptive protection. Extra contraceptive method required during and for at least 4 weeks after stopping rifampicin.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":45,"q":"K⁺ 6.8, ECG: peaked T waves, prolonged PR. First treatment step?","a":["IV furosemide","IV calcium gluconate","Oral Kayexalate","Hemodialysis"],"ex":"In severe hyperkalemia with ECG changes: first calcium gluconate for cardioprotection. Then actively lower K⁺.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"Hyperkalemia (K⁺ >5.5 mmol/L) disrupts the resting membrane potential of cardiac cells → increased risk of life-threatening arrhythmias. ECG progression: peaked T waves → widened PR → wide QRS → sine wave → ventricular fibrillation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Causes","desc":"Renal insufficiency (most common), ACE inhibitors/ARBs, potassium-sparing diuretics, adrenal insufficiency, tissue breakdown (rhabdomyolysis, TLS)."},{"title":"Acidosis effect","desc":"Acidosis shifts K⁺ from intracellular to extracellular space (per 0.1 pH decrease: K⁺ rises ~0.5 mmol/L)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"True hyperkalemia with ECG changes","desc":"K⁺ >6 + peaked T waves, wide QRS → treat immediately: calcium gluconate + K⁺ lowering."},{"label":"Pseudohyperkalemia","desc":"Hemolysis in the blood tube gives falsely elevated K⁺ — ECG normal. Always repeat if in doubt."},{"label":"Asymptomatic hyperkalemia","desc":"Elevated K⁺ without ECG changes — less acute but requires monitoring and treatment of the cause."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IV calcium gluconate — membrane stabilization, works in 1-3 min.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Glucose + insulin","detail":"IV glucose + insulin → K⁺ shifts into intracellular space (works in 15-30 min)."},{"naam":"Salbutamol","detail":"Nebulizer (beta-2 stimulation) → additional K⁺ shift into cells."},{"naam":"Bicarbonate","detail":"Sodium bicarbonate in metabolic acidosis — correction shifts K⁺ intracellularly."},{"naam":"Definitive","detail":"Resonium, Kayexalate or hemodialysis for definitive K⁺ removal."}]},{"_i":46,"q":"ABG:\npH 7.49\npCO₂ 30 mmHg\nHCO₃⁻ 22 mmol/L\npO₂ 98 mmHg\nCorrect interpretation?","a":["Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation","Mixed alkalosis","Metabolic acidosis with overcompensation"],"ex":"High pH + low pCO₂ = respiratory alkalosis. HCO₃ slightly decreased = renal compensation (expected: 24 − 0.5×ΔpCO₂ acute). Causes: hyperventilation, sepsis (early), pregnancy, pulmonary embolism, salicylate intoxication.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":47,"q":"Microcytic anemia:\nFerritin 6 µg/L\nTransferrin saturation 5%\nSerum iron 5 µmol/L\nReticulocytes low\nDiagnosis?","a":["β-thalassemia minor","Anemia of chronic disease","Iron deficiency anemia","Sideroblastic anemia"],"ex":"Iron deficiency anemia: low ferritin (<12), transferrin saturation <16%, low serum iron. Ferritin is an acute-phase protein — in inflammation it can be falsely normal. Distinguish from anemia of chronic disease: in chronic anemia ferritin is normal/high.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"Iron deficiency anemia is the most common anemia worldwide. Iron is needed for hemoglobin synthesis.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Iron is absorbed in the small intestine (as Fe²⁺, promoted by vitamin C)."},{"title":"Causes of deficiency","desc":"too little intake (vegans, malnutrition), malabsorption (celiac disease, IBD, gastrectomy), increased loss (menstruation, GI bleeding)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Ferritin is the storage form — depleted first."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Anemia of chronic disease (ACD)","desc":"ferritin normal/high, serum iron low, transferrin low — iron trapped in macrophages by inflammation."},{"label":"Thalassemia","desc":"microcytic but ferritin/serum iron normal, Hb electrophoresis abnormal."},{"label":"Sideroblastic anemia","desc":"ringed sideroblasts in bone marrow."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Treat the cause (GI bleeding? celiac disease?).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Oral ferrous sulfate 200 mg/day (or ferrous fumarate) — 3-6 months to replenish iron stores."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"IV iron (ferric carboxymaltose) in malabsorption, intolerance or urgent situation."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Reticulocytes rise after 5-7 days as the first sign of response."}]},{"_i":48,"q":"Patient on acenocoumarol: INR 3.8, APTT normal. Which clotting factor is NOT affected?","a":["Factor II (prothrombin)","Factor VII","Factor VIII","Factor X"],"ex":"Vitamin K antagonists inhibit factors II, VII, IX and X (vitamin K-dependent). Factor VIII is vitamin K-independent. PT/INR reflects factor VII (shortest half-life). Normal APTT confirms: intrinsic pathway intact.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":49,"q":"After chemotherapy:\nK⁺ 6.4 mmol/L\nPhosphate 3.2 mmol/L\nUrate 870 µmol/L\nCalcium 1.6 mmol/L\nLDH 2900 U/L\nDiagnosis?","a":["Tumor lysis syndrome (TLS)","Nephrotic syndrome","Addisonian crisis","Rhabdomyolysis"],"ex":"TLS: massive cell death → release of cell contents → hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, hyperuricemia, hypocalcemia (phosphate binds Ca²⁺). Risk: kidney failure and life-threatening arrhythmias. Prevention: hyperhydration + rasburicase/allopurinol.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":50,"q":"A normal serum B12 level completely rules out functional vitamin B12 deficiency.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Serum B12 reflects total (active + inactive transcobalamin). Methylmalonic acid and homocysteine are functional markers — elevated in intracellular B12 deficiency despite normal serum level. MMA is specific for B12 deficiency.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":51,"q":"Creatinine acutely increased: 188 µmol/L\nUrine Na 9 mmol/L\nUrine osmolality 680 mosm/kg\nMost likely cause?","a":["Acute tubular necrosis (ATN)","Prerenal kidney failure","Postrenal obstruction","Acute glomerulonephritis"],"ex":"Prerenal: tubules still function → maximal Na reabsorption (urine Na <20) + water reabsorption (osmolality >500). ATN: tubules damaged → urine Na >40, osmolality <350. Prerenal treatment: fluid resuscitation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"Prerenal kidney failure is functional: the tubules work normally but there is too little flow. The kidneys respond correctly: maximally retaining Na and water.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Prerenal: low renal perfusion."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"RAAS + ADH."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"urine Na <20, osmolality >500, FENa <1%. ATN: tubules dead."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"urine Na >40, osmolality <350, FENa >2%. FENa = (urine Na × plasma creatinine) / (plasma Na × urine creatinine) × 100 — correct for diuretic use!."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Postrenal obstruction","desc":"ultrasound shows hydronephrosis."},{"label":"Glomerulonephritis","desc":"proteinuria, hematuria, red blood cell casts."},{"label":"Contrast nephropathy","desc":"rise in creatinine 24-48 h after contrast, ATN pattern."},{"label":"Hepatorenal syndrome","desc":"in cirrhosis, functional (low FENa) but does NOT respond to fluid supplementation."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":52,"q":"A prolonged APTT with normal PT without bleeding symptoms can indicate a lupus anticoagulant.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Lupus anticoagulant prolongs APTT in vitro (inhibits phospholipid-dependent reactions) — but paradoxically causes thrombotic risk in vivo. Mixing study: no correction (= inhibitor present). Confirmation: dRVVT test. Treatment: anticoagulation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":53,"q":"28-year-old woman: anemia, indirect bilirubin elevated, LDH 880, haptoglobin undetectable, blood smear: schistocytes. Diagnosis?","a":["Iron deficiency anemia","Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA)","Thalassemia","G6PD deficiency"],"ex":"MAHA: schistocytes (fragments of damaged red blood cells) + hemolysis parameters (high LDH, indirect bili, low haptoglobin). Causes: TTP, HUS, DIC, HELLP. Urgent diagnosis — TTP treatment is plasmapheresis.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"Microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (MAHA) is a pattern in which red blood cells are mechanically torn apart in small blood vessels (thrombotic microangiopathy). Schistocytes on blood smear are the pathognomonic sign — this is always abnormal.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Thrombotic occlusions in small vessels (TTP: ADAMTS13 deficiency."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"von Willebrand multimers accumulate; HUS: Shiga toxin damages endothelium; DIC: systemic coagulation activation). RBCs are literally cut apart by this."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"schistocytes, hemoglobin released."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"high LDH, low haptoglobin."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"TTP","desc":"pentad = MAHA + thrombocytopenia + neurological deficits + kidney failure + fever."},{"label":"HUS (typical)","desc":"child after Campylobacter/EHEC O157, predominantly kidney failure."},{"label":"HELLP","desc":"pregnancy, liver dysfunction, hypertension."},{"label":"DIC","desc":"underlying cause (sepsis, malignancy), abnormal coagulation tests (prolonged PT/APTT)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"TTP: URGENT plasmapheresis (removes von Willebrand multimers + supplies ADAMTS13).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Caplacizumab as adjuvant."},{"naam":"Atypical HUS","detail":"eculizumab."},{"naam":"HELLP","detail":"delivery is definitive treatment."},{"naam":"DIC","detail":"treat underlying cause + supplement clotting factors."}]},{"_i":54,"q":"In atrial fibrillation with a CHA₂DS₂-VASc score of 0 in a man, anticoagulation is indicated.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. CHA₂DS₂-VASc 0 (man) = low risk → no anticoagulation recommended. Score counts: heart failure, hypertension, age ≥75 (2 points), diabetes, stroke/TIA (2 points), vascular disease, age 65-74, female sex.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"CHA₂DS₂-VASc calculates annual stroke risk in atrial fibrillation. Man with score 0 = risk <1%/year → anticoagulation causes more harm than benefit. Anticoagulation: men ≥2 points, women ≥3 points.","wiki_redflag":"Women: female sex is a risk-enhancing factor, not an independent indication — a woman with only female sex (score 1) receives NO anticoagulation.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Stroke risk in AF","desc":"Stasis in the left atrial appendage → thrombus → embolism to cerebral vessels. CHA₂DS₂-VASc quantifies this risk based on comorbidities."},{"title":"Bleeding risk (HAS-BLED)","desc":"Opposite the anticoagulation benefit is bleeding risk. HAS-BLED score (Hypertension, Abnormal renal/liver function, Stroke, Bleeding, Labile INR, Elderly, Drugs/alcohol) helps map this."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Score 0 (man): no anticoagulation","desc":"Annual stroke risk <1% — anticoagulation-induced bleeding risk > stroke prevention benefit. No treatment indicated."},{"label":"Score ≥2 (man) or ≥3 (woman)","desc":"DOAC (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran or edoxaban) unless contraindicated. VKA only with mechanical heart valve or mitral stenosis."},{"label":"Score 1 (man)","desc":"Gray area — consider anticoagulation, decide individually, prefer DOAC if clinical benefit present."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute AF <48 h: cardioversion possible without anticoagulation (or after TEE to rule out thrombus).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Rate control","detail":"Beta-blocker or non-DHP calcium channel blocker for rate control (<110/min at rest)."},{"naam":"Anticoagulation","detail":"DOAC based on CHA₂DS₂-VASc — start after bleeding risk assessment (HAS-BLED)."}]},{"_i":55,"q":"55-year-old woman with exertional shortness of breath, orthopnea and swollen ankles. Echo: EF 35%, enlarged LV. What is first-line treatment?","a":["Diuretics only","ACE inhibitor + beta-blocker","Digoxin monotherapy","Calcium channel blocker"],"ex":"Heart failure with reduced EF (HFrEF): ACE inhibitor (or ARB/ARNI) + beta-blocker reduce mortality. Diuretics relieve symptoms. Digoxin only for symptom control. Add spironolactone with persistent symptoms.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"HFrEF (EF <40%) is systolic heart failure — the heart pumps insufficiently. Four drug classes each independently reduce mortality.","wiki_redflag":"Calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) are contraindicated in HFrEF — negative inotropic effect worsens pump function.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Reduced contraction","desc":"LV myocardium damaged (ischemia, viral, idiopathic) → EF falls → RAAS/sympathetic nervous system activated."},{"title":"Neurohormonal activation","desc":"Compensatory RAAS and adrenaline → salt/water retention and higher heart rate — useful in the short term."},{"title":"Vicious circle","desc":"Chronic RAAS activation → myocardial fibrosis, remodeling → EF falls further."},{"title":"Medication breaks the circle","desc":"ACE-i/beta-blocker/MRA/SGLT2i each block an arm of neurohormonal activation → reverse remodeling."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"HFrEF (EF <40%)","desc":"Systolic dysfunction. Four-pillar therapy indicated with proven mortality reduction."},{"label":"HFpEF (EF ≥50%)","desc":"Diastolic dysfunction. Only SGLT2 inhibitors show benefit — ACE-i/beta-blocker do not."},{"label":"Acute pulmonary edema","desc":"Life-threatening decompensation of HFrEF — oxygen + IV furosemide + nitrates urgently."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start four-pillar therapy as soon as hemodynamically tolerated after stabilization.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ACE inhibitor/ARNI","detail":"Lisinopril or sacubitril-valsartan (ARNI superior in stable HFrEF) — lowers afterload and RAAS."},{"naam":"Beta-blocker","detail":"Carvedilol, bisoprolol or metoprolol succinate — start only in stable patient, low and titrate up."},{"naam":"MRA","detail":"Spironolactone 25–50 mg — potassium-sparing diuretic with additional mortality reduction (RALES trial)."},{"naam":"SGLT2 inhibitor","detail":"Dapagliflozin or empagliflozin — most recent pillar, works independently of the other three."}]},{"_i":56,"q":"In a STEMI, PCI (balloon angioplasty) should ideally take place within 90 minutes after first medical contact.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. "Door-to-balloon time" <90 min is the guideline. The faster, the more myocard saved. Alternative: thrombolysis within 30 min if PCI is not available in time (door-to-needle <30 min).","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"STEMI time targets: "Time is muscle." Primary PCI is the gold standard — every additional 30 minutes of delay = ~7.5% more myocardial loss. Door-to-balloon <90 minutes is the norm.","wiki_redflag":"Thrombolysis is reserved if PCI is not reachable within 120 minutes after first medical contact — door-to-needle then <30 minutes. After lysis: transfer to PCI center within 2-24 h for coronary angiography.","wiki_bigfact_label":"minutes","wiki_bigfact_sub":"door-to-balloon time target in STEMI (primary PCI)","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Myocardial loss per unit time","desc":"Complete coronary occlusion → 1 g myocardium per minute in the risk zone dies. After 90 minutes, on average 30-40 g of myocardium is necrotic. PCI restores perfusion and salvage of myocardium is time-critical."},{"title":"PCI vs thrombolysis","desc":"Primary PCI: higher TIMI 3 flow, less re-occlusion, fewer bleedings than thrombolysis. Thrombolysis: faster to start but limited to first 12 h, bleeding contraindications."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"STEMI: PCI within 90 minutes","desc":"Gold standard. Door-to-balloon <90 min after first medical contact. Give dual antiplatelet therapy (aspirin + ticagrelor/prasugrel) immediately."},{"label":"Thrombolysis as alternative","desc":"Alteplase if PCI not available within 120 min. Door-to-needle <30 min. Absolute CI: previous intracranial hemorrhage, active bleeding, severe hypertension."},{"label":"NSTEMI/unstable angina","desc":"No ST elevation — less urgent, coronary angiography within 24-72 h depending on risk score (GRACE score)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Directly to catheterization lab — no unnecessary diagnostics or delaying consultation.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Immediate aspirin + ticagrelor","detail":"Aspirin 300 mg + ticagrelor 180 mg (or prasugrel 60 mg if PCI plan) orally on arrival."},{"naam":"IV heparin","detail":"70-100 IU/kg bolus for PCI — periprocedural anticoagulation."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"After STEMI: high-intensity statin + beta-blocker + ACE inhibitor + SGLT2 inhibitor."}]},{"_i":57,"q":"35-year-old man after flu: sharp precordial pain, worse lying down, better leaning forward. ECG: saddle-shaped ST elevation in multiple leads. Diagnosis?","a":["STEMI","Pericarditis","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection"],"ex":"Pericarditis: pleuritic pain (sharp, positional), pericardial friction rub, diffuse saddle-shaped ST elevation. Cause often viral. Treatment: ibuprofen + colchicine. Complication: pericardial tamponade.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium. The pain is sharp and pleuritic — worse with inspiration and lying flat, better sitting forward. Cause is viral in 80-90% (Coxsackie, EBV, CMV).","wiki_redflag":"Tamponade: if fluid accumulates quickly, as little as 200 ml can be fatal — Beck's triad: hypotension + distended neck veins + muffled heart sounds.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Pericardial inflammation","desc":"Inflammation makes the pericardial layers rough → friction rub on auscultation (characteristic, "creaky leather")."},{"title":"ECG pattern","desc":"Diffuse saddle-shaped ST elevation (all leads) + PR depression — unlike STEMI, no regional distribution and no reciprocal ST depression."},{"title":"Fluid accumulation","desc":"More inflamed pericardium produces exudate → pericardial effusion → rub disappears but tamponade risk increases."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"Sharp positional pain, diffuse saddle-shaped ST + PR depression, pain better leaning forward, friction rub."},{"label":"STEMI","desc":"Localized ST elevation (regional distribution), reciprocal depressions, pressing pain — no positional effect."},{"label":"Myocarditis","desc":"Also post-viral but troponin strongly elevated, pain less pronounced, diffuse LV dysfunction on echo."},{"label":"Pulmonary embolism","desc":"Pleuritic pain + dyspnea, S1Q3T3 on ECG, D-dimer elevated — no saddle-shaped ST."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Ibuprofen 600 mg 3×/day + colchicine 0.5 mg 2×/day — start immediately, for 3 months (recurrence prevention).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Rest","detail":"No exertion until symptom-free for 3 months — sport increases arrhythmia risk in active pericardial inflammation."},{"naam":"Recurrence","detail":"Corticosteroids if NSAID + colchicine fail — use cautiously (higher recurrence risk when tapering)."},{"naam":"Tamponade","detail":"Urgent pericardiocentesis in hemodynamic compromise (echo-guided, subxiphoid access)."}]},{"_i":58,"q":"Patient with known pericarditis: acutely in shock, distended neck veins, muffled heart sounds. What is the diagnosis?","a":["Pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism","Cardiac tamponade","Heart failure"],"ex":"Cardiac tamponade: Beck's triad = hypotension + distended neck veins + muffled heart sounds. Fluid in the pericardial sac compresses the heart. Echo: echo-free space + diastolic collapse of right ventricles. Treatment: urgent pericardiocentesis.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Cardiac tamponade occurs when fluid (blood, exudate) accumulates in the pericardial space quickly enough to impair diastolic filling of the heart. As little as 200 ml of rapidly accumulating fluid can be fatal; 1 liter chronically can be well tolerated.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Pericardial compression","desc":"Accumulating fluid in the pericardial space compresses the RV during diastole — the RV can no longer expand."},{"title":"Reduced filling","desc":"Less RV filling → less stroke volume → less LV filling → cardiac output falls rapidly."},{"title":"Compensatory response","desc":"Sympathetic activation → tachycardia + vasoconstriction. But this maintains output only temporarily."},{"title":"Pulsus paradoxus","desc":"During inspiration, intrathoracic pressure falls → RV fills better but compresses the LV → systolic BP falls >10 mmHg. Characteristic of tamponade."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Cardiac tamponade","desc":"Beck's triad: hypotension + distended neck veins + muffled heart sounds. Pulsus paradoxus >10 mmHg. Echo: free fluid + RV collapse in diastole."},{"label":"RV infarction","desc":"Also low BP + high JVP but no muffled heart sounds, ST elevation in V4R, no pericardial effusion on echo."},{"label":"Tension pneumothorax","desc":"Also low BP + distended JVP but unilateral absent breath sounds, hyperresonant percussion, tracheal deviation."},{"label":"Cardiogenic shock","desc":"Cold extremities + pulmonary edema + low BP but no distended neck veins or muffled heart sounds."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Urgent pericardiocentesis: subxiphoid needle, echo-guided drainage.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"In hemopericardium (trauma)","detail":"Surgical drainage (pericardiocentesis sometimes insufficient with active bleeding)."},{"naam":"Investigate cause","detail":"After stabilization: malignancy, TB, viral pericarditis, uremia or autoimmune disease as possible etiology."},{"naam":"Clinical recovery","detail":"Even a small drainage volume (50-100 ml) gives rapid hemodynamic recovery — the effect is remarkably fast."}]},{"_i":59,"q":"ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. ACE inhibitors are actually first-choice drugs in HFrEF — they reduce mortality. Contraindications are: bilateral renal artery stenosis, pregnancy, and hypersensitivity (e.g. angioedema).","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"ACE inhibitors are not contraindicated but indicated in HFrEF. They reduce RAAS activation and improve survival by ~20%. The only absolute contraindications: bilateral renal artery stenosis, pregnancy, history of angioedema.","wiki_redflag":"True ACE inhibitor contraindications: bilateral renal artery stenosis (acute worsening of kidney function), pregnancy (fetotoxic), prior ACE inhibitor angioedema (switch to ARB). Dry cough → switch to ARB (also proven in HFrEF).","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"RAAS blockade","desc":"ACE inhibitors block conversion of angiotensin I to II → less aldosterone → less sodium/water retention + less vasoconstriction → lower preload and afterload."},{"title":"Reverse remodeling","desc":"Chronic RAAS activation in HFrEF leads to myocardial fibrosis and further EF decline. ACE inhibitors break this cycle → reverse remodeling → EF rises on average 5-10% after 3-6 months."},{"title":"Mortality reduction","desc":"CONSENSUS and SOLVD studies: enalapril reduced mortality in HFrEF by 16-40%. Combination with beta-blocker + MRA + SGLT2i gives additive reduction."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ACE inhibitors: first choice in HFrEF","desc":"Indicated in all HFrEF patients unless true contraindication. Mortality reduction 16-27% in randomized studies."},{"label":"ARB: alternative for cough/angioedema","desc":"Valsartan, candesartan or losartan if ACE inhibitor not tolerated. No cough (no bradykinin effect). CHARM study: proven in HFrEF."},{"label":"ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan)","desc":"Superior to ACE inhibitor (PARADIGM-HF: 20% mortality reduction vs enalapril). Recommended as next step in stable HFrEF patients already using ACE inhibitor/ARB."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start as soon as hemodynamically tolerated after stabilization.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Start low","detail":"Begin with low dose (e.g. enalapril 2.5 mg bid), increase to target dose (enalapril 10-20 mg bid) over 2-4 weeks."},{"naam":"Monitor renal function","detail":"Creatinine + potassium after 1-2 weeks — creatinine rise <30% is acceptable. K⁺ rise >5.5 mmol/L → reduce dose."}]},{"_i":60,"q":"72-year-old woman: grade 4/6 systolic murmur right parasternal, radiating to neck, exertional syncope, shortness of breath. Echo: AV area 0.7 cm². Diagnosis?","a":["Mitral regurgitation","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Severe aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"ex":"Severe aortic stenosis: AV area <1.0 cm², classic triad = angina + syncope + shortness of breath. Systolic ejection murmur right parasternal. Treatment: TAVI or surgical valve replacement.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Aortic valve stenosis is the most common valvular abnormality in older adults. The aortic valve becomes progressively stiffer and narrower due to calcification → the heart must pump increasingly hard → LV hypertrophy → eventually heart failure.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Calcification of the aortic valve","desc":"Inflammatory damage to the valve leaflet (similar to atherosclerosis) → progressive calcification → valve area falls <1.0 cm² (severe: <0.6 cm²). Risk factors: age, bicuspid aortic valve, renal insufficiency."},{"title":"LV overload","desc":"Increased afterload → LV must squeeze harder → pressure peak rises → concentric LV hypertrophy as compensation."},{"title":"Diastolic dysfunction","desc":"Hypertrophic LV wall is stiffer → impaired diastolic filling → elevated filling pressure → exertional dyspnea."},{"title":"Classic triad","desc":"Angina (insufficient coronary reserve), syncope (reflex bradycardia with exertion or low output), dyspnea (elevated filling pressure). Average survival after triad: 2-3 years without valve replacement."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Severe aortic stenosis","desc":"AV area <1.0 cm², classic triad syncope + angina + dyspnea, right parasternal murmur radiating to neck."},{"label":"HOCM","desc":"Also syncope + murmur in young people but murmur increases with Valsalva (reduced filling). Aortic stenosis murmur decreases with Valsalva."},{"label":"Mitral regurgitation","desc":"Holosystolic murmur, radiating to axilla, maximal at apex."},{"label":"Pulmonary hypertension","desc":"Dyspnea + syncope but loud P2, no right parasternal ejection murmur."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Aortic valve replacement (surgical or TAVI) is the only curative treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"TAVI","detail":"Transcatheter aortic valve implantation for older adults and high-surgical-risk patients — less invasive than surgery, comparable outcomes."},{"naam":"Conservative","detail":"No proven pharmacological therapy that slows progression — medication relieves symptoms but does not cure."},{"naam":"Caveat nitrates/diuretics","detail":"Can cause dangerous hypotension — the compensated heart is preload-dependent."}]},{"_i":61,"q":"ECG: PR interval progressively lengthens until a QRS drops, then resets. Which conduction disorder?","a":["First-degree AV block","Wenckebach (second-degree type I)","Mobitz type II AV block","Third-degree AV block"],"ex":"Wenckebach (Mobitz type I): PR progressively prolonged → QRS drop → reset. Usually benign, often in inferior wall infarction. Mobitz II: sudden QRS drop without PR prolongation — more dangerous, can lead to complete block.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"AV blocks are classified by severity. First-degree: prolonged PR, always conduction.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Wenckebach: AV node fatigue","desc":"After every conducted beat, the AV node becomes temporarily 'tired' → conduction time lengthens → PR interval becomes longer with each beat."},{"title":"Wenckebach: QRS drop and reset","desc":"After an impulse is not conducted, the AV node recovers completely → PR interval starts short again. This repeats cyclically."},{"title":"Mobitz II: His bundle damage","desc":"Structural damage below the AV node (His bundle or bundle branches) → sudden, unexpected QRS drop without preceding PR prolongation — more dangerous than Wenckebach."},{"title":"Third-degree: complete block","desc":"No impulse conducted from atria to ventricles → AV dissociation. Ventricles beat on their own escape rhythm (30-40/min) → hemodynamically unstable."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Wenckebach (Mobitz I)","desc":"Progressively lengthening PR → QRS drop → reset. Benign, typical in inferior MI (RCA ischemia of AV node), also with increased vagal tone."},{"label":"First-degree AV block","desc":"PR >200 ms but every P conducts to QRS. Asymptomatic, no treatment in athletes or vagal effect."},{"label":"Mobitz II","desc":"Constant PR until sudden QRS drop — structural His bundle damage, risk of complete block → urgent pacemaker."},{"label":"Complete (third-degree) AV block","desc":"P and QRS completely dissociated — urgent pacemaker always indicated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"First-degree: none.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Wenckebach","detail":"observation, treat underlying cause (ischemia, medication)."},{"naam":"Mobitz II","detail":"temporary pacemaker, then permanent."},{"naam":"Third-degree","detail":"urgent transvenous temporary pacemaker, then permanent."}]},{"_i":62,"q":"Statins lower LDL cholesterol by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. HMG-CoA reductase is the key enzyme in cholesterol synthesis in the liver. Inhibition → less intracellular cholesterol → more LDL receptors → more LDL uptake from blood. Adverse effect: myopathy (check CK with muscle pain).","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"HMG-CoA reductase is the rate-limiting enzyme in endogenous cholesterol synthesis. Inhibition → liver makes less cholesterol → compensates with more LDL receptors → LDL uptake from blood increases.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"LDL reduction","wiki_bigfact_sub":"With high-intensity statin (atorvastatin 40-80 mg or rosuvastatin 20-40 mg).","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"HMG-CoA inhibition","desc":"Statins block the conversion of HMG-CoA to mevalonate — the first step in cholesterol synthesis. This lowers intracellular cholesterol content of the hepatocyte."},{"title":"Upregulation of LDL receptors","desc":"Lower intracellular cholesterol → SREBP-2 activated → more LDL receptor expression → more LDL uptake from plasma. Result: LDL falls 30-55%."},{"title":"Pleiotropic effects","desc":"Besides LDL lowering: stabilize plaques, improve endothelial function, lower CRP. Partly responsible for cardiovascular protection even with normal LDL."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Statins (HMG-CoA inhibitors)","desc":"First choice. High-intensity (atorva 40-80 mg, rosuva 20-40 mg): >50% LDL reduction. Low-intensity (prava 10-20 mg): 20-30%."},{"label":"Ezetimibe","desc":"Inhibits intestinal cholesterol absorption (NPC1L1). Combination with statin: additional 15-20% LDL reduction. Well tolerated."},{"label":"PCSK9 inhibitors (evolocumab, alirocumab)","desc":"Block PCSK9 → more LDL receptors → extreme LDL reduction (50-60% on top of statin). Indication: FH or high risk with insufficient statin effect."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"After MI: start high-intensity statin immediately (atorvastatin 80 mg or rosuvastatin 40 mg), regardless of baseline LDL.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Adverse effect myopathy","detail":"Stop statin if CK >10× normal (rhabdomyolysis). With muscle pain without CK rise: switch to another statin or lower dose."},{"naam":"Monitoring","detail":"Liver enzymes at baseline — routine monitoring no longer recommended unless symptoms."}]},{"_i":63,"q":"22-year-old young man faints on the sports field. Father died suddenly at 38. Systolic murmur that increases with Valsalva. Diagnosis?","a":["Aortic stenosis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","Mitral valve prolapse","Wolff-Parkinson-White"],"ex":"HCM: asymmetric septal hypertrophy, autosomal dominant. Murmur increases with Valsalva (less filling → more obstruction). Risk of sudden cardiac death in young people. Treatment: beta-blocker, ICD if high risk, sports prohibited.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"HCM (hypertrophic cardiomyopathy) is the most common inherited heart disease (1:500) and the most common cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes. Autosomal dominant mutation in sarcomere proteins (MYH7, MYBPC3).","wiki_redflag":"The murmur of HCM INCREASES with Valsalva and standing (less filling → more obstruction) — this is the opposite of aortic stenosis, in which the murmur decreases.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","desc":"Thickened interventricular septum (IVS) obstructs the LVOT (outflow tract) during systole → dynamic pressure gradient."},{"title":"SAM (systolic anterior motion)","desc":"Venturi effect pulls the anterior mitral leaflet toward the IVS → worsens LVOT obstruction + mitral regurgitation. Visible on echo."},{"title":"Dynamic obstruction","desc":"Gradient increases with ↓ preload (standing, Valsalva, dehydration) and ↓ afterload. Decreases with ↑ preload (squatting, handgrip test). This is why nitrates are dangerous."},{"title":"Arrhythmia risk","desc":"Disorganized muscle fibers + fibrosis → re-entry circuits → VT/VF → syncope or sudden cardiac death, even in young athletes without prior history."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy (HCM)","desc":"Murmur increases with Valsalva/standing. Asymmetric IVS >15 mm on echo. SAM of mitral valve. Family history of sudden cardiac death."},{"label":"Aortic stenosis","desc":"Murmur decreases with Valsalva (fixed mechanical obstruction). Older patient. Calcification on echo. No familial sudden cardiac death."},{"label":"WPW","desc":"Syncope due to sudden tachyarrhythmia, delta wave + short PR on ECG. No septal hypertrophy on echo."},{"label":"Mitral valve prolapse","desc":"Midsystolic click + late systolic murmur. Benign condition, rarely sudden cardiac death."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Beta-blocker (first choice) — lowers heart rate → longer filling time → less obstruction.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"ICD indication","detail":"With ≥1 risk factor: survived VT/VF, syncope, familial sudden cardiac death, septum ≥30 mm, NSVT, hypotensive response to exertion."},{"naam":"Sports restriction","detail":"Competitive sports prohibited — exertion dramatically increases LVOT gradient and arrhythmia risk."},{"naam":"Septal reduction","detail":"Surgical myectomy or alcohol ablation for refractory LVOT gradient >50 mmHg despite medication."}]},{"_i":64,"q":"52-year-old man: chest pain 3 hours ago. First troponin normal. What is the correct management?","a":["Discharge: first troponin normal","Repeat troponin after 3-6 hours","Start PCI immediately","Repeat ECG only"],"ex":"Troponin rises only 3-6 hours after infarction. One normal value does not rule out NSTEMI. Repeat after 3-6 hours (or 1-2 hours with high-sensitivity troponin). Combine with ECG and clinical picture. Never let the patient go too early.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Hs-troponin rises only 1-3 hours after infarction and peaks at 12-24 hours. One normal troponin with symptoms <3 h does NOT rule out NSTEMI — serial measurement is mandatory.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"ESC protocol","wiki_bigfact_sub":"Measure on arrival and after 1 hour — combination of absolute value + delta rapidly rules ACS in or out.","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Troponin release kinetics","desc":"Troponin T and I leak from ischemic cardiomyocytes — detection with hs-assay after 1-3 h, peak 12-24 h, normalizes 5-14 days."},{"title":"Serial measurement","desc":"0+1 h ESC protocol: high absolute hs-troponin at t=0 = rule-in. Low at t=0 and t=1 h = rule-out (NPV >99%). Significant rise (delta) = rule-in ACS."},{"title":"False-negative risk","desc":"With symptoms <3 h: troponin may still be normal despite ongoing infarction. Early discharge based on one measurement is dangerous."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Serial hs-troponin (0+1 h or 0+3 h)","desc":"Standard protocol in suspected ACS in the ED. Always combine with ECG and clinical picture."},{"label":"Elevated troponin without ACS","desc":"PE, myocarditis, sepsis, renal failure, cardiomyopathy — troponin is cardiac-specific but not ACS-specific. Clinical context is decisive."},{"label":"HEART score","desc":"History + ECG + Age + Risk + Troponin: risk stratification ≤3 = low risk (0.9% MACE), ≥7 = high risk (50% MACE). Helps determine discharge indication."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"STEMI: immediate PCI. NSTEMI: aspirin + ticagrelor, heparin, coronary angiography within 2-72 h depending on risk.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Low-risk NSTEMI","detail":"GRACE score <140 + stable: conservative management with coronary angiography within 72 h."},{"naam":"High-risk NSTEMI","detail":"GRACE score ≥140 or refractory symptoms: urgent coronary angiography within 2-24 h."}]}]}

